lic event id — read the whole event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`, or find it in `GET /v1/events?accountId=...`. The title, date and summary beside it are copies taken when the point of view was written, so fetch the event when you need its current form. That fetch can 404: the point of view is not rewritten when an event it cites is dismissed afterwards."},"eventTitle":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date","description":"The date the event happened, `YYYY-MM-DD` — not a timestamp."},"eventSummary":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"connection":{"type":"string","description":"One line on how THIS event supports the prediction — the causal or strategic link, not a restatement of the title."}},"required":["eventId","eventTitle","eventDate","eventSummary","connection"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The events the prediction rests on, at least one and at most three. A prediction with no evidence is never written, so this is never empty."}},"required":["point","evidence"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"At most three, strongest first. Empty when the events do not support a direction — an honest answer, not a failure."},"whyNow":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal whose recent firing makes the timing matter. Matches a `signalKey` on `GET /v1/signals` and on the cited events."},"point":{"type":"string","description":"One line on why the timing matters now."},"events":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"eventId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"A public event id — read the whole event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`, or find it in `GET /v1/events?accountId=...`. The title, date and summary beside it are copies taken when the point of view was written, so fetch the event when you need its current form. That fetch can 404: the point of view is not rewritten when an event it cites is dismissed afterwards."},"eventTitle":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date","description":"The date the event happened, `YYYY-MM-DD` — not a timestamp."}},"required":["eventId","eventTitle","eventDate"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The last few events that matched this signal, most recent first."}},"required":["signalKey","point","events"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"At most three. Always empty when `predictions` is empty — timing means nothing without a direction it is timely for."},"generatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time","description":"When this point of view was written."}},"required":["predictions","whyNow","generatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Trayo's reading of what this account is doing and why it matters to what your workspace sells — the \"why\" behind the account, where `scores.activity.summary` is the \"why\" behind the number. Every prediction cites the events it rests on, so you can check the reading against the record instead of taking it on trust.\n\nNull until the first discovery run produces events and the point of view is generated; regenerated when new events arrive. Also null when the stored copy predates the current shape — an out-of-date reading is withheld rather than served in pieces. Reading this route never generates one."},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"identity":{"type":"string","enum":["resolved","uncertain","not_found","pending"],"description":"A prediction of which company this account is, from its `url`, checked while the import ran. Discovery runs its own match for each new account afterwards, and that match can land on a different company or on none. Over MCP, `trayo_get_account_matches` reads that final result; over REST, a discovery run lists any account it could not identify in `blockedSignals`. `resolved`: the website belongs to the company in `matchedCompany`. `uncertain`: the website carries several possible companies and none clearly owns it; `alternatives` lists them. Discovery may pick the wrong one or none. `not_found`: Trayo holds no company for this website, so discovery will fail with `company_not_found` unless you correct the account. `pending`: not checked here: the row has no `url`, the row carries a `linkedinHandle` that the website check did not confirm (the handle is tried first later and may still succeed), or the check did not answer in time. Pending accounts are matched in the background. Fix an `uncertain` or `not_found` account with `PATCH /v1/accounts/{id}` (`trayo_update_account` over MCP): send the company's own website or its `linkedinHandle`. New values may be added; treat an unknown one as `pending`."},"matchedCompany":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"The company id. Send it in `filters.companyIds` on `POST /v1/find` to search this exact company."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That company, as Trayo names it. Compare it with the name you sent."},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Its headcount, when known."},"linkedinHandle":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That company's LinkedIn handle, when Trayo has one. Send it as the account's `linkedinHandle` to pin the identity."}},"required":["id","name","headcount","linkedinHandle"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Only for `identity: \"resolved\"`: the company the website belongs to. Null otherwise."},"alternatives":{"anyOf":[{"maxItems":3,"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"The company id. Send it in `filters.companyIds` (instead of `filters.companyWebsites`) to search this exact company."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That company, as Trayo names it."},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Its headcount, when known."}},"required":["id","name","headcount"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Only for `identity: \"uncertain\"`: the companies this website could name, at most 3. Null otherwise."}},"required":["id","name","url","logoUrl","linkedinHandle","starred","scores","pointOfView","createdAt","updatedAt","identity","matchedCompany","alternatives"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The rows that were created, in submission order."},"skipped":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"0-based position of the offending row in the submitted array."},"reason":{"type":"string","description":"Why this row was not created, in the same wording the single-create route would have returned for the same failure. PROSE — do not branch on it; branch on `reasonCode`."},"reasonCode":{"description":"The machine-readable class of the skip, present only for the classes a caller has to act on differently. `duplicate` means the row collided with a record you already have (or with an earlier row in the same batch) — `existingId` names it, and the remedy is a `PATCH`, not a re-post. `identity_conflict` means the submitted LinkedIn identity and email identify two different people; send one identity, or PATCH the intended record explicitly. Every other skip is a problem with the row itself: this field is ABSENT and `reason` says what to fix.","type":"string","enum":["duplicate","identity_conflict"]},"existingId":{"description":"The record this row collided with. Present exactly when `reasonCode` is `duplicate`, so a caller can `PATCH` the existing record instead of guessing which of its rows already existed. It may name a row created EARLIER IN THIS SAME batch.","type":"string","format":"uuid"}},"required":["index","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Rows that were NOT created. Always inspect this: a `200` means the call succeeded, not that every row landed."}},"required":["created","skipped"],"description":"**Partial success is the contract.** A batch of 40 with 3 bad rows creates 37 and reports the other 3 in `skipped` by their position in the submitted array. It never fails the whole request for a subset of bad rows, and the status is always `200` even when every row was skipped. Only a problem with the REQUEST itself — missing auth, the array missing or not an array, or more than 200 items — fails the call.","additionalProperties":false},"UpdateAccountDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string","maxLength":200},"url":{"type":"string","format":"uri"},"linkedinHandle":{"type":"string","maxLength":200},"starred":{"type":"boolean"}},"description":"Every field is optional; only fields present in the body are changed. `starred` is the one field settable here but not at creation. A `linkedinHandle` is reduced to the bare company slug before it is stored, so sending the full company URL is safe.","additionalProperties":false},"SignalListDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"data":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The stable public identifier. There is no separate row id."},"type":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"entityType":{"type":"string"},"detects":{"type":"string"},"salesIntent":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"includes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"weight":{"type":"number"},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"}},"required":["signalKey","type","entityType","detects","salesIntent","includes","excludes","weight","createdAt","updatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Every current account-axis signal, newest first."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","const":false,"description":"ALWAYS `false`. This route does not paginate."},"nextCursor":{"type":"null","description":"ALWAYS `null`. This route does not paginate."}},"required":["data","hasMore","nextCursor"],"description":"The complete current set of your workspace's account-axis signals in a single response — every signal you have authored.\n\n**This route does NOT paginate**, unlike `GET /v1/accounts`, `GET /v1/people`, `GET /v1/events` and `GET /v1/discoveries`. It accepts no `cursor` or `limit` (any sent are ignored), and `hasMore` is always `false` with `nextCursor` always `null` however many signals exist. Do not write a pagination loop against it — it will never advance. `POST /v1/find` is the other non-paginating route, and it does not even return this envelope.","additionalProperties":false},"CreateSignalDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{1,58}[a-z0-9]$","description":"Stable public identifier, lowercase alphanumeric with underscores or hyphens (`champion_move`, `funding-round`), 3-60 characters, starting and ending with a letter or digit. This is the signal's identity — it appears in the path of every other signal route and cannot be renamed. A key that is already taken is a 409 (`signal_key_conflict`); a reserved product-managed key is a 400."},"type":{"type":"string","enum":["news","jobs","job_change"],"description":"What this signal searches. `news` searches the open web. `jobs` finds hiring activity. `job_change` finds people who started a new role. While the run is active, keep polling it. After it settles, read `blockedSignals` and `error` before reporting an empty result.\n\n`technology` is system-managed and not authorable. `reddit` is not authorable here either: those signals require a competitor list and this route has no field for one."},"detects":{"type":"string","maxLength":2000,"description":"Describe what counts as a match in plain language."},"salesIntent":{"description":"Why a match matters commercially.","type":"string","maxLength":2000},"includes":{"description":"Prose hints that widen the match.","maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludes":{"description":"Prose hints that narrow the match.","maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"weight":{"description":"Relative importance, 1-3. Default 1.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":3}},"required":["signalKey","type","detects"],"description":"A new signal is usable immediately: send its key to `POST /v1/discoveries` as soon as this call answers. No further call is needed before starting discovery.","additionalProperties":false},"SignalDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The stable public identifier. There is no separate row id."},"type":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"entityType":{"type":"string"},"detects":{"type":"string"},"salesIntent":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"includes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"weight":{"type":"number"},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"}},"required":["signalKey","type","entityType","detects","salesIntent","includes","excludes","weight","createdAt","updatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"UpdateSignalDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"detects":{"type":"string","maxLength":2000},"salesIntent":{"type":"string","maxLength":2000},"includes":{"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludes":{"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"weight":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":3}},"description":"Every field is optional; only fields present are changed. `signalKey` and `type` are absent on purpose — the key is the resource's identity and sending either is a `validation_failed`.\n\nAn edit takes effect on the next run that names the signal.","additionalProperties":false},"SignalDeleteResultDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"deleted":{"type":"boolean","const":true},"signalKey":{"type":"string"}},"required":["deleted","signalKey"],"description":"Confirmation, NOT the deleted signal — the response shape differs from every other delete in this API. The `signalKey` becomes immediately reusable in a new `POST /v1/signals` call.","additionalProperties":false},"StartDiscoveryDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"accountIds":{"minItems":1,"maxItems":500,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$","format":"uuid"},"description":"Accounts to scan, 1 to 500. Every id must exist in your workspace — an unknown one is a 400 (`unknown_account`) listing the offenders, not a silent skip."},"lookbackDays":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":365,"description":"How far back to look for events, in days."},"maxResults":{"description":"Requested source-result cap per account and signal, 1 to 1000, before filtering and deduplication; this is not a target number of matching events. Omit it to use each source's default — 25 for jobs, posts and people searches. A number raises or lowers those defaults. Google News account searches always use a fixed 10-result budget and ignore `maxResults`. Send a number or omit the field — `null` is rejected.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":1000},"signalKeys":{"description":"Omit to run every signal in the workspace. You can use a new signal key immediately. A key that does not exist is a different thing and is a 400 (`unknown_signal`). Send at least one key or omit the field — an EMPTY array is rejected, because it reads like \"no signals\" and used to mean \"all of them\". Each selected signal searches its enabled sources for every account. More accounts, signals and source results mean more work and a longer wait. Select only the signal keys relevant to the task; omitting this field can make a large run take much longer.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"people":{"default":"none","description":"`none` (default): this run adds no people to your workspace; its events come back with `people: []`. A `job_change` event still names the person who changed jobs; add them with POST /v1/people if you want them. `add`: the people who matter to each event are found, added to your workspace and listed on the event's `people` as the run goes. Find and add people yourself any time with POST /v1/find and POST /v1/people/batch.","type":"string","enum":["add","none"]}},"required":["accountIds","lookbackDays"],"additionalProperties":false},"DiscoveryRunDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"status":{"type":"string","enum":["running","completed","failed"],"description":"`running`, `completed` or `failed`. This is the POLLING signal and nothing else: it tells you when to stop calling, not what the run achieved. Read `error` and `partial` even on a `completed` run.\n\n**`completed` means everything this run will ever write has been written.** It covers both halves of the run: the search across your accounts, and the step that turns what the search found into events. It flips at the same instant `settledAt` is set, and from that instant the candidate and event counts are frozen and `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` returns the events that stand for the run's scope: everything it wrote, at any event date, plus matches an earlier run wrote. There is nothing to wait for afterwards.\n\n**`running` covers the write step too**, so a run whose search has finished still reads `running` while its events are being written. That is why `completedAt` can be set while `status` is `running`: the search is done, the run is not. Poll on `settledAt` (or on this field reaching `completed`, which is the same moment) and never on `completedAt`. A run that finds nothing reaches `completed` exactly as promptly as one that finds a hundred events."},"target":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string","const":"accounts"},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"signalKeys":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},{"type":"null"}]}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The accounts and signals requested for this run. Older runs can omit these details."},"lookbackDays":{"type":"number"},"maxResults":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The requested source-result cap this run was started with, before filtering and deduplication. `null` means none was sent, so each source used its default. Google News account searches always use a fixed 10-result budget and ignore `maxResults`; this value is not an event count."},"people":{"type":"string","enum":["add","none"],"description":"The `people` option this run was started with: `add` or `none`. A run started before the option existed reads `add`, which is what it did."},"accountCount":{"type":"number","description":"How many accounts the run was asked to scan."},"candidatesScreened":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many candidate items this run RETRIEVED and screened: articles, posts and profiles pulled from the sources for your accounts, before anything judged whether they match a signal. **It is not a count of events, and these candidates cannot be read as events.** `candidatesMatched` says how many of them matched a signal, `eventsNew` how many new events the run wrote, and the events themselves are read with `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=`. `candidatesScreened: 12` with `candidatesMatched: 0` means 12 items were screened and none matched any signal, so this execution wrote no events and there is nothing of its own to read. The same number as the deprecated `eventsFound`.\n\n**Final once `settledAt` is set**, and still moving before that.\n\n**`candidatesScreened: 0` on a RE-RUN does not mean nothing was searched.** Items a signal has already seen are dropped before they are stored, so a second identical run reports 0 even though the same sources were queried and returned the same items: on any later run this number says how much was NEW to that signal, not how much was looked at. To tell \"nothing was searched\" from \"nothing was new\", use `blockedSignals` and `error`, which do not decay on re-runs."},"candidatesMatched":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How many of the screened candidates matched a signal and went into an event: a new event, or an event already in your workspace that absorbed them. Several matched candidates can make one event, so this is not an event count either; `eventsNew` is. `candidatesMatched` above zero with `eventsNew: 0` means what matched was already known: the scoped events from `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` hold it. The sum of `bySignal[].candidatesMatched`. Final once `settledAt` is set. Null only on a run settled before this field existed."},"bySignal":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal."},"candidatesScreened":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Candidates this signal retrieved and screened in this run. Not events."},"candidatesMatched":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many of those matched this signal and went into an event, new or already in your workspace. 0 means the signal matched nothing in this run."}},"required":["signalKey","candidatesScreened","candidatesMatched"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"How many candidates one signal screened in this run, and how many of them matched it."}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The screened and matched candidates per signal the run covered, sorted by `signalKey`. Every signal is listed, including one that screened or matched nothing, so a signal that matched no account reads `candidatesMatched: 0` here rather than being absent. A signal with `candidatesScreened: 0` may not have searched at all: check `blockedSignals` and `failedChecks` for it. Empty when the run searched nothing (`error` says why). Final once `settledAt` is set. Null only on a run settled before this field existed."},"eventsFound":{"type":"number","deprecated":true,"description":"**Deprecated: read `candidatesScreened`, the same number under a name that says what it counts.** Kept, with the same value, for callers that already read it. It counts candidate items retrieved and screened, NOT events: `eventsFound: 12` with no events to read means 12 candidates were screened and none matched (`candidatesMatched: 0`), not 12 events that are missing."},"eventsNew":{"type":"number","description":"How many new events THIS RUN WROTE to your workspace. It is the run's execution receipt, not the size of `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=`. That list answers the customer's question instead: every live event this run wrote, whatever its event date, plus every one standing for the run's accounts, signals and lookback window, including events written by earlier runs. It can therefore be non-empty, or longer than this count, when `eventsNew` is zero — and on a settled run, read to the end, it is never shorter than this count except where an event has since been withdrawn or deleted. One page is capped at 100, so compare against everything `nextCursor` yields, never against a single page.\n\nThe list can also be shorter. A safety net downstream of discovery can withdraw an event after the run ends, and a caller can delete one; the frozen receipt still says the run wrote it. Explicit `state=dismissed` reads Trayo's withdrawals in the same scope, while caller-deleted events remain absent.\n\n**Act on it once `settledAt` is set, and not before.** Until then the run is still writing events and this number is still rising: a value read from an unsettled run is a partial count, not a small result. Once `settledAt` is set the number is frozen — it is the run's receipt, so it stays what it was even if you later delete some of the events the run created.\n\n**`eventsNew: 0` on a `completed` run is a success, not a failure.** Results are deduplicated against everything already in the workspace, so RE-RUNNING AN IDENTICAL DISCOVERY IS SAFE: the same accounts, signals and lookback typically return `eventsNew: 0` on the second call and create no duplicate events. Do not treat a zero here as a reason to retry. Read the scoped events page, `error`, and `blockedSignals`; do not branch on `status` alone — a `completed` run always means the work finished, whatever it found."},"blockedSignals":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal that produced nothing here, and could not have."},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["account_not_ready","company_not_found"],"description":"Machine-readable cause. Use this field to choose the next action. These outcomes apply to every signal type, including `news`. While a run is active, keep polling the same run. `account_not_ready` means Trayo could not search the account this time. After the run settles, retry the affected account IDs. `company_not_found` means Trayo could not identify the company from the supplied details. For `company_not_found`, check the named accounts before starting another discovery. If an identity is wrong, send the correct `linkedinHandle` with `trayo_update_account` over MCP, or with `PATCH /v1/accounts/{id}` and a workspace API key over REST. When no handle is known, send a corrected website `url` instead. Both need `accounts:write`. `trayo_update_account` also reports whether Trayo can now identify the company. Importing the same hostname again skips the existing account and does not update it. After the current run settles, start a new discovery for the corrected account IDs."},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"description":"WHICH accounts this signal did not search, so a re-run can name exactly those instead of every account in the run. Truncated to the first 200; `accountsAffected` is always the true total, so `accountIds.length < accountsAffected` means there are more, and the next run reports what is still blocked."},"accountsAffected":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many of the accounts in this run the signal cannot search for this reason. Never truncated, unlike `accountIds`."},"accountsRequested":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many accounts the run was asked to scan, so you can tell a partial blackout from a total one. Equal values mean this signal contributes nothing to this run at all."},"message":{"type":"string","description":"The same thing in prose, including what to do next."}},"required":["signalKey","reason","accountIds","accountsAffected","accountsRequested","message"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"A signal that cannot produce results for part of this run, and why."},"description":"Signals that did not search some accounts. Each entry identifies the affected accounts in `accountIds` and explains the cause in `reason`. Read this field and `error` before reporting an empty result. These reasons alone do not mark the whole run as failed. These outcomes apply to every signal type, including `news`. While a run is active, keep polling the same run. `account_not_ready` means Trayo could not search the account this time. After the run settles, retry the affected account IDs. `company_not_found` means Trayo could not identify the company from the supplied details. For `company_not_found`, check the named accounts before starting another discovery. If an identity is wrong, send the correct `linkedinHandle` with `trayo_update_account` over MCP, or with `PATCH /v1/accounts/{id}` and a workspace API key over REST. When no handle is known, send a corrected website `url` instead. Both need `accounts:write`. `trayo_update_account` also reports whether Trayo can now identify the company. Importing the same hostname again skips the existing account and does not update it. After the current run settles, start a new discovery for the corrected account IDs."},"partial":{"type":"boolean","description":"True when the results are INCOMPLETE: at least one account × signal check failed (`failedChecks` lists them), or the run was closed with work still outstanding (`error` says what). `status` stays `completed` on such a run — it says the run stopped, not that every search succeeded. Read `failedChecks` for which signals and accounts, and do not report `eventsNew: 0` on a partial run as \"nothing happened\". Can turn true while the run is still going; final once `settledAt` is set."},"failedChecks":{"type":"object","properties":{"count":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Account × signal checks that did not finish. A run over 50 accounts and 10 signals makes 500 checks."},"bySignal":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal whose search did not finish for these accounts."},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["timeout","source_unavailable","internal"],"description":"`timeout`: a data source took too long to answer. `source_unavailable`: a data source refused or dropped the request. `internal`: anything else. The first two are transient; re-running the same accounts and signal later is the fix."},"failedAccounts":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many accounts this signal did not finish searching for this reason. Never truncated."},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"description":"Which accounts, so a re-run can name exactly those. Truncated to the first 200; `failedAccounts` is always the true total."}},"required":["signalKey","reason","failedAccounts","accountIds"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"One signal that did not finish searching some accounts, and why."},"description":"The failed checks grouped by signal and reason. Empty on a clean run."}},"required":["count","bySignal"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Searches this run STARTED and could not finish — a data source timed out or failed. Unlike `blockedSignals`, which lists searches that could not start, these accounts were eligible and simply went unsearched for these signals. An empty result for them is not evidence that nothing happened."},"progress":{"type":"object","properties":{"accountsFinished":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Accounts that no longer await a result: those that reported back, whatever they found, and those that could not be searched at all (`error` and `blockedSignals` say which). Every account, once the run has settled."},"accountsTotal":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Accounts the run was asked to scan."},"etaSeconds":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Estimated seconds until `settledAt` is set. `0` once the run has settled. `null` when there is no estimate yet: in the first 30 seconds, before a tenth of the accounts have finished, or once the final write step has run longer than expected. Projected from the share of accounts finished so far and bounded by this workspace's recent runs of a similar size. An estimate, not a promise: the run is final only when `settledAt` is set."},"estimatedSettleAt":{"description":"When the run is expected to settle: the time of this read plus `etaSeconds`. Equal to `settledAt` once the run has settled; `null` whenever `etaSeconds` is.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"retryAfterSeconds":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How long to wait before reading this run again: close to `etaSeconds`, between 10 and 300 seconds, and 30 when there is no estimate. The same value is sent as the `Retry-After` header. `null` once the run has settled, because there is nothing left to wait for. Do other work in between rather than polling faster."}},"required":["accountsFinished","accountsTotal","etaSeconds","estimatedSettleAt","retryAfterSeconds"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"How far the run has come. Equal account numbers mean no account is still being searched; the run is final only when `settledAt` is set, because events are still being written after the last account answers."},"retry":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["retry_later"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`retry_later` on a settled partial run whose failures were transient (a `timeout` or `source_unavailable` check, or work cut short): re-running the same accounts and signals unchanged, later, is the fix. Null otherwise — a clean run needs no retry, a run still going has not finished failing, and a run whose every failure is `internal` would fail the same way."},"startedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"completedAt":{"description":"When the SEARCH finished — every account in the run reported back. **It is not the end of the run and it is not what to poll on:** the events the search found are written afterwards, so this can be set while `status` is still `running`. Wait for `settledAt`. Null until the search finishes, then stable.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"settledAt":{"description":"THE FINISH LINE — when the run became final: the search finished AND every event it produced has been written. **Act when `settledAt` is set.** Poll `GET /v1/discoveries/{runId}` until this field is non-null; at that moment `status` reads `completed` or `failed`, the candidate and event counts are frozen for good, and `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` returns every live event this run wrote — at any event date, since a job change is dated by the month the job STARTED and can predate the lookback — plus every one standing for the run's account × signal × lookback scope, including events an earlier run wrote. The counts remain this run's frozen receipt. Do not poll on `completedAt`, and do not try to detect the end by watching `eventsNew` stop changing — a pause between two writes looks exactly like the end of the run and is not one. Null until the run settles, then stable.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"error":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Read this even when `status` is `completed`. A completed run with a non-null `error` ended without doing everything it was asked to — most commonly because none of the requested accounts was eligible (every account must be assigned to a user), which is the single most likely reason a first-time caller sees no new events. The scoped events page can still contain matches written before this run, so this field remains the authority on whether this execution failed."}},"required":["id","status","target","lookbackDays","maxResults","people","accountCount","candidatesScreened","candidatesMatched","bySignal","eventsFound","eventsNew","blockedSignals","partial","failedChecks","progress","retry","startedAt","completedAt","settledAt","error"],"description":"A discovery run. Discovery is ASYNCHRONOUS: `POST /v1/discoveries` returns 202 immediately, then you poll `GET /v1/discoveries/{runId}` until `settledAt` is non-null — the point at which `status` reads `completed` or `failed` and the counts stop moving — then read the scoped events, `error`, `blockedSignals`, and `partial`/`failedChecks`.","additionalProperties":false},"DiscoveryRunListDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"data":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"status":{"type":"string","enum":["running","completed","failed"],"description":"`running`, `completed` or `failed`. This is the POLLING signal and nothing else: it tells you when to stop calling, not what the run achieved. Read `error` and `partial` even on a `completed` run.\n\n**`completed` means everything this run will ever write has been written.** It covers both halves of the run: the search across your accounts, and the step that turns what the search found into events. It flips at the same instant `settledAt` is set, and from that instant the candidate and event counts are frozen and `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` returns the events that stand for the run's scope: everything it wrote, at any event date, plus matches an earlier run wrote. There is nothing to wait for afterwards.\n\n**`running` covers the write step too**, so a run whose search has finished still reads `running` while its events are being written. That is why `completedAt` can be set while `status` is `running`: the search is done, the run is not. Poll on `settledAt` (or on this field reaching `completed`, which is the same moment) and never on `completedAt`. A run that finds nothing reaches `completed` exactly as promptly as one that finds a hundred events."},"target":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string","const":"accounts"},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"signalKeys":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},{"type":"null"}]}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The accounts and signals requested for this run. Older runs can omit these details."},"lookbackDays":{"type":"number"},"maxResults":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The requested source-result cap this run was started with, before filtering and deduplication. `null` means none was sent, so each source used its default. Google News account searches always use a fixed 10-result budget and ignore `maxResults`; this value is not an event count."},"people":{"type":"string","enum":["add","none"],"description":"The `people` option this run was started with: `add` or `none`. A run started before the option existed reads `add`, which is what it did."},"accountCount":{"type":"number","description":"How many accounts the run was asked to scan."},"candidatesScreened":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many candidate items this run RETRIEVED and screened: articles, posts and profiles pulled from the sources for your accounts, before anything judged whether they match a signal. **It is not a count of events, and these candidates cannot be read as events.** `candidatesMatched` says how many of them matched a signal, `eventsNew` how many new events the run wrote, and the events themselves are read with `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=`. `candidatesScreened: 12` with `candidatesMatched: 0` means 12 items were screened and none matched any signal, so this execution wrote no events and there is nothing of its own to read. The same number as the deprecated `eventsFound`.\n\n**Final once `settledAt` is set**, and still moving before that.\n\n**`candidatesScreened: 0` on a RE-RUN does not mean nothing was searched.** Items a signal has already seen are dropped before they are stored, so a second identical run reports 0 even though the same sources were queried and returned the same items: on any later run this number says how much was NEW to that signal, not how much was looked at. To tell \"nothing was searched\" from \"nothing was new\", use `blockedSignals` and `error`, which do not decay on re-runs."},"candidatesMatched":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How many of the screened candidates matched a signal and went into an event: a new event, or an event already in your workspace that absorbed them. Several matched candidates can make one event, so this is not an event count either; `eventsNew` is. `candidatesMatched` above zero with `eventsNew: 0` means what matched was already known: the scoped events from `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` hold it. The sum of `bySignal[].candidatesMatched`. Final once `settledAt` is set. Null only on a run settled before this field existed."},"bySignal":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal."},"candidatesScreened":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Candidates this signal retrieved and screened in this run. Not events."},"candidatesMatched":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many of those matched this signal and went into an event, new or already in your workspace. 0 means the signal matched nothing in this run."}},"required":["signalKey","candidatesScreened","candidatesMatched"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"How many candidates one signal screened in this run, and how many of them matched it."}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The screened and matched candidates per signal the run covered, sorted by `signalKey`. Every signal is listed, including one that screened or matched nothing, so a signal that matched no account reads `candidatesMatched: 0` here rather than being absent. A signal with `candidatesScreened: 0` may not have searched at all: check `blockedSignals` and `failedChecks` for it. Empty when the run searched nothing (`error` says why). Final once `settledAt` is set. Null only on a run settled before this field existed."},"eventsFound":{"type":"number","deprecated":true,"description":"**Deprecated: read `candidatesScreened`, the same number under a name that says what it counts.** Kept, with the same value, for callers that already read it. It counts candidate items retrieved and screened, NOT events: `eventsFound: 12` with no events to read means 12 candidates were screened and none matched (`candidatesMatched: 0`), not 12 events that are missing."},"eventsNew":{"type":"number","description":"How many new events THIS RUN WROTE to your workspace. It is the run's execution receipt, not the size of `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=`. That list answers the customer's question instead: every live event this run wrote, whatever its event date, plus every one standing for the run's accounts, signals and lookback window, including events written by earlier runs. It can therefore be non-empty, or longer than this count, when `eventsNew` is zero — and on a settled run, read to the end, it is never shorter than this count except where an event has since been withdrawn or deleted. One page is capped at 100, so compare against everything `nextCursor` yields, never against a single page.\n\nThe list can also be shorter. A safety net downstream of discovery can withdraw an event after the run ends, and a caller can delete one; the frozen receipt still says the run wrote it. Explicit `state=dismissed` reads Trayo's withdrawals in the same scope, while caller-deleted events remain absent.\n\n**Act on it once `settledAt` is set, and not before.** Until then the run is still writing events and this number is still rising: a value read from an unsettled run is a partial count, not a small result. Once `settledAt` is set the number is frozen — it is the run's receipt, so it stays what it was even if you later delete some of the events the run created.\n\n**`eventsNew: 0` on a `completed` run is a success, not a failure.** Results are deduplicated against everything already in the workspace, so RE-RUNNING AN IDENTICAL DISCOVERY IS SAFE: the same accounts, signals and lookback typically return `eventsNew: 0` on the second call and create no duplicate events. Do not treat a zero here as a reason to retry. Read the scoped events page, `error`, and `blockedSignals`; do not branch on `status` alone — a `completed` run always means the work finished, whatever it found."},"blockedSignals":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal that produced nothing here, and could not have."},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["account_not_ready","company_not_found"],"description":"Machine-readable cause. Use this field to choose the next action. These outcomes apply to every signal type, including `news`. While a run is active, keep polling the same run. `account_not_ready` means Trayo could not search the account this time. After the run settles, retry the affected account IDs. `company_not_found` means Trayo could not identify the company from the supplied details. For `company_not_found`, check the named accounts before starting another discovery. If an identity is wrong, send the correct `linkedinHandle` with `trayo_update_account` over MCP, or with `PATCH /v1/accounts/{id}` and a workspace API key over REST. When no handle is known, send a corrected website `url` instead. Both need `accounts:write`. `trayo_update_account` also reports whether Trayo can now identify the company. Importing the same hostname again skips the existing account and does not update it. After the current run settles, start a new discovery for the corrected account IDs."},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"description":"WHICH accounts this signal did not search, so a re-run can name exactly those instead of every account in the run. Truncated to the first 200; `accountsAffected` is always the true total, so `accountIds.length < accountsAffected` means there are more, and the next run reports what is still blocked."},"accountsAffected":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many of the accounts in this run the signal cannot search for this reason. Never truncated, unlike `accountIds`."},"accountsRequested":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many accounts the run was asked to scan, so you can tell a partial blackout from a total one. Equal values mean this signal contributes nothing to this run at all."},"message":{"type":"string","description":"The same thing in prose, including what to do next."}},"required":["signalKey","reason","accountIds","accountsAffected","accountsRequested","message"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"A signal that cannot produce results for part of this run, and why."},"description":"Signals that did not search some accounts. Each entry identifies the affected accounts in `accountIds` and explains the cause in `reason`. Read this field and `error` before reporting an empty result. These reasons alone do not mark the whole run as failed. These outcomes apply to every signal type, including `news`. While a run is active, keep polling the same run. `account_not_ready` means Trayo could not search the account this time. After the run settles, retry the affected account IDs. `company_not_found` means Trayo could not identify the company from the supplied details. For `company_not_found`, check the named accounts before starting another discovery. If an identity is wrong, send the correct `linkedinHandle` with `trayo_update_account` over MCP, or with `PATCH /v1/accounts/{id}` and a workspace API key over REST. When no handle is known, send a corrected website `url` instead. Both need `accounts:write`. `trayo_update_account` also reports whether Trayo can now identify the company. Importing the same hostname again skips the existing account and does not update it. After the current run settles, start a new discovery for the corrected account IDs."},"partial":{"type":"boolean","description":"True when the results are INCOMPLETE: at least one account × signal check failed (`failedChecks` lists them), or the run was closed with work still outstanding (`error` says what). `status` stays `completed` on such a run — it says the run stopped, not that every search succeeded. Read `failedChecks` for which signals and accounts, and do not report `eventsNew: 0` on a partial run as \"nothing happened\". Can turn true while the run is still going; final once `settledAt` is set."},"failedChecks":{"type":"object","properties":{"count":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Account × signal checks that did not finish. A run over 50 accounts and 10 signals makes 500 checks."},"bySignal":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string","description":"The signal whose search did not finish for these accounts."},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["timeout","source_unavailable","internal"],"description":"`timeout`: a data source took too long to answer. `source_unavailable`: a data source refused or dropped the request. `internal`: anything else. The first two are transient; re-running the same accounts and signal later is the fix."},"failedAccounts":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many accounts this signal did not finish searching for this reason. Never truncated."},"accountIds":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"description":"Which accounts, so a re-run can name exactly those. Truncated to the first 200; `failedAccounts` is always the true total."}},"required":["signalKey","reason","failedAccounts","accountIds"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"One signal that did not finish searching some accounts, and why."},"description":"The failed checks grouped by signal and reason. Empty on a clean run."}},"required":["count","bySignal"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Searches this run STARTED and could not finish — a data source timed out or failed. Unlike `blockedSignals`, which lists searches that could not start, these accounts were eligible and simply went unsearched for these signals. An empty result for them is not evidence that nothing happened."},"progress":{"type":"object","properties":{"accountsFinished":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Accounts that no longer await a result: those that reported back, whatever they found, and those that could not be searched at all (`error` and `blockedSignals` say which). Every account, once the run has settled."},"accountsTotal":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Accounts the run was asked to scan."},"etaSeconds":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Estimated seconds until `settledAt` is set. `0` once the run has settled. `null` when there is no estimate yet: in the first 30 seconds, before a tenth of the accounts have finished, or once the final write step has run longer than expected. Projected from the share of accounts finished so far and bounded by this workspace's recent runs of a similar size. An estimate, not a promise: the run is final only when `settledAt` is set."},"estimatedSettleAt":{"description":"When the run is expected to settle: the time of this read plus `etaSeconds`. Equal to `settledAt` once the run has settled; `null` whenever `etaSeconds` is.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"retryAfterSeconds":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How long to wait before reading this run again: close to `etaSeconds`, between 10 and 300 seconds, and 30 when there is no estimate. The same value is sent as the `Retry-After` header. `null` once the run has settled, because there is nothing left to wait for. Do other work in between rather than polling faster."}},"required":["accountsFinished","accountsTotal","etaSeconds","estimatedSettleAt","retryAfterSeconds"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"How far the run has come. Equal account numbers mean no account is still being searched; the run is final only when `settledAt` is set, because events are still being written after the last account answers."},"retry":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["retry_later"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`retry_later` on a settled partial run whose failures were transient (a `timeout` or `source_unavailable` check, or work cut short): re-running the same accounts and signals unchanged, later, is the fix. Null otherwise — a clean run needs no retry, a run still going has not finished failing, and a run whose every failure is `internal` would fail the same way."},"startedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"completedAt":{"description":"When the SEARCH finished — every account in the run reported back. **It is not the end of the run and it is not what to poll on:** the events the search found are written afterwards, so this can be set while `status` is still `running`. Wait for `settledAt`. Null until the search finishes, then stable.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"settledAt":{"description":"THE FINISH LINE — when the run became final: the search finished AND every event it produced has been written. **Act when `settledAt` is set.** Poll `GET /v1/discoveries/{runId}` until this field is non-null; at that moment `status` reads `completed` or `failed`, the candidate and event counts are frozen for good, and `GET /v1/events?discoveryRunId=` returns every live event this run wrote — at any event date, since a job change is dated by the month the job STARTED and can predate the lookback — plus every one standing for the run's account × signal × lookback scope, including events an earlier run wrote. The counts remain this run's frozen receipt. Do not poll on `completedAt`, and do not try to detect the end by watching `eventsNew` stop changing — a pause between two writes looks exactly like the end of the run and is not one. Null until the run settles, then stable.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"error":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Read this even when `status` is `completed`. A completed run with a non-null `error` ended without doing everything it was asked to — most commonly because none of the requested accounts was eligible (every account must be assigned to a user), which is the single most likely reason a first-time caller sees no new events. The scoped events page can still contain matches written before this run, so this field remains the authority on whether this execution failed."}},"required":["id","status","target","lookbackDays","maxResults","people","accountCount","candidatesScreened","candidatesMatched","bySignal","eventsFound","eventsNew","blockedSignals","partial","failedChecks","progress","retry","startedAt","completedAt","settledAt","error"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"A discovery run. Discovery is ASYNCHRONOUS: `POST /v1/discoveries` returns 202 immediately, then you poll `GET /v1/discoveries/{runId}` until `settledAt` is non-null — the point at which `status` reads `completed` or `failed` and the counts stop moving — then read the scoped events, `error`, `blockedSignals`, and `partial`/`failedChecks`."},"description":"Discovery runs, most recently started first."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"Another page exists. When `false` you have reached the end — stop requesting pages."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Pass verbatim as the next request's `cursor` to fetch the next page. Null exactly when `hasMore` is false."}},"required":["data","hasMore","nextCursor"],"additionalProperties":false},"EventListDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"data":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountName":{"type":"string","description":"The account this event is about, named. On every event, expanded or not, so a digest can say which company without a second call — there is no route that turns an `accountId` back into a company."},"accountUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uri"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That account's website as an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL, or `null` when the workspace does not know one — same field, and same rule, as `url` on `GET /v1/accounts`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"summary":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"whyItMatters":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why this event matters to YOUR workspace — one short paragraph relating the event to your solutions and the signals it matched, written by discovery when it wrote the event. `summary` says what happened; this says why you should care. It is the same text the Trayo app shows under \"Why it matters\" and that webhook payloads carry as `whyItMatters`.\n\n`null` when discovery did not produce one — older events, and events whose signal type skips that stage. Never an empty string."},"offerings":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"The name your workspace gave the offering."},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Why that offering is relevant to this event, in one line."},"link":{"type":"string","description":"Where to read more about the offering. `\"\"` when none was set."}},"required":["name","description","link"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Which of YOUR solutions this event relates to — the offerings from your workspace profile that discovery tied to the event when it wrote `whyItMatters`. The same array webhook payloads carry as `offerings`.\n\n`null`, never `[]`, when discovery tied none to it: older events, events whose signal type skips that stage, and workspaces with no offerings on their profile (`PATCH /v1/workspace`)."},"url":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"publication":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"signalKeys":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Which of YOUR signals matched this event, by `signalKey` — the slug you named the signal, the same value `POST /v1/discoveries` takes in its `signalKeys` and `?signalKey=` filters on here. On EVERY event, compact reads included, sorted and without repeats so two reads of one event agree.\n\n**This is the per-event attribution `signalType` does not give you.** `signalType` is the kind of source a signal reads — `news`, `jobs`, `job_change` — and your signals usually share a handful of those between them; `signalKeys` names the signals themselves. So a run over many signals stays attributable event by event, and a run per signal is not needed to tell which one fired.\n\nUsually one key. Several when several of your signals matched the same event, which Trayo records on the one event rather than duplicating it. `[]` only where no signal is linked at all: an event from before these links were recorded, or one whose signal has since been deleted.\n\n`expand=signals` carries the same keys with each match's `type`, `confidence` and `evidence`; this field is the keys alone, and it costs no expansion."},"jobChange":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"type":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How the person moved — for example `new_hire` or `promotion`."},"date":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"When the move happened, as discovery read it off the profile."},"previousRole":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"company":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"since":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["title","company","since"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where they came from. `null` when discovery found no prior role."}},"required":["type","date","previousRole"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The move behind a `job_change` event — the same object webhook payloads carry as `jobChange`.\n\n`null` on every event whose `signalType` is not `job_change`. On a `job_change` event the object is always present, and its own fields read `null` where discovery could not classify the move."},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The only two states this API publishes. An event that has not reached one of them is not addressable yet — it 404s.\n\n`dismissed` means Trayo withdrew an event it had produced. To dismiss one yourself send `DELETE /v1/events/{id}`; the event then reads back in neither state."},"dismissalReason":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why Trayo withdrew this event — a short machine-readable tag such as `stale_job_change`, `no_signal_match` or `within_batch_duplicate:{eventId}`. The vocabulary is Trayo's own and grows over time, so read it as a label, not an enum.\n\nOnly ever set on an event whose `state` is `dismissed`, and only when a reason was recorded. `null` on every `live` event."},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"people":{"description":"Present only when `expand` was sent on the list route. Always present on `GET /v1/events/{id}`.\n\n**It is EMPTY until three preconditions hold, and an empty array here does not mean nobody was relevant.** Attaching people to an EVENT is a stage of its own. It runs only when (1) the account is ready — the same readiness `blockedSignals` reports on a discovery run, which the account reaches on its own shortly after it is created — (2) the workspace has a stakeholder definition, and (3) a discovery run you start yourself was started with `people: \"add\"`. The default, `people: \"none\"`, adds no people. A `job_change` event still names the person who changed jobs; add them with `POST /v1/people` if you want them.\n\n(2) and (3) are yours: `PATCH /v1/workspace`, and `people: \"add\"` on the run. All must be in place BEFORE the discovery run that produces the event — none is applied retroactively — so a workspace that sets its definition after its first runs gets `[]` on the events those runs produced and people on the ones after. Set it first.\n\nThe people listed here are already in your workspace: `id` is a person id you can pass to list and contact tools without adding them again. Importing an account adds no people; to add people at an account yourself, search with `POST /v1/find` and add the ones you choose with `POST /v1/people/batch`. `signals` expands normally.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why this person is relevant to this event."}},"required":["id","fullName","title","linkedinUrl","photoUrl","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}},"signals":{"description":"How well each of your signals matched, and from what reasoning. Same expansion rule as `people`. Send `expand` for that per-match detail, not to learn WHICH signals fired — every event names those in `signalKeys`, expanded or not.\n\nOne entry per stored match, ordered by `signalKey` like `signalKeys`, but NOT deduplicated the way that field is: a signal you have refined can hold two matches on one event, one per version of it. Such repeats carry IDENTICAL `confidence` and `evidence`, because the detail is looked up by `signalKey` — so a repeated key tells you nothing extra. Expect repeats and read `signalKeys` for the distinct set.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string"},"type":{"type":"string"},"confidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["low","medium","high"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How sure the match was, on the three-step scale the matcher writes: `low`, `medium`, `high`. `null` when the run did not record one for this signal — older events, and signals attached by a stage that does not score its matches."},"evidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why this signal matched THIS event, in the matcher's own words — the sentence or two it reasoned from. `whyItMatters` argues the event to your workspace; this shows the work behind one signal.\n\n`null` when the run recorded no reasoning for this signal. Never an empty string."}},"required":["signalKey","type","confidence","evidence"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["id","accountId","accountName","accountUrl","title","summary","whyItMatters","offerings","url","publication","eventDate","signalType","signalKeys","jobChange","state","dismissalReason","createdAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Events, most recently created first."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"Another page exists. When `false` you have reached the end — stop requesting pages."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Pass verbatim as the next request's `cursor` to fetch the next page. Null exactly when `hasMore` is false."}},"required":["data","hasMore","nextCursor"],"additionalProperties":false},"EventDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountName":{"type":"string","description":"The account this event is about, named. On every event, expanded or not, so a digest can say which company without a second call — there is no route that turns an `accountId` back into a company."},"accountUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uri"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That account's website as an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL, or `null` when the workspace does not know one — same field, and same rule, as `url` on `GET /v1/accounts`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"summary":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"whyItMatters":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why this event matters to YOUR workspace — one short paragraph relating the event to your solutions and the signals it matched, written by discovery when it wrote the event. `summary` says what happened; this says why you should care. It is the same text the Trayo app shows under \"Why it matters\" and that webhook payloads carry as `whyItMatters`.\n\n`null` when discovery did not produce one — older events, and events whose signal type skips that stage. Never an empty string."},"offerings":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"The name your workspace gave the offering."},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Why that offering is relevant to this event, in one line."},"link":{"type":"string","description":"Where to read more about the offering. `\"\"` when none was set."}},"required":["name","description","link"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Which of YOUR solutions this event relates to — the offerings from your workspace profile that discovery tied to the event when it wrote `whyItMatters`. The same array webhook payloads carry as `offerings`.\n\n`null`, never `[]`, when discovery tied none to it: older events, events whose signal type skips that stage, and workspaces with no offerings on their profile (`PATCH /v1/workspace`)."},"url":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"publication":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"signalKeys":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Which of YOUR signals matched this event, by `signalKey` — the slug you named the signal, the same value `POST /v1/discoveries` takes in its `signalKeys` and `?signalKey=` filters on here. On EVERY event, compact reads included, sorted and without repeats so two reads of one event agree.\n\n**This is the per-event attribution `signalType` does not give you.** `signalType` is the kind of source a signal reads — `news`, `jobs`, `job_change` — and your signals usually share a handful of those between them; `signalKeys` names the signals themselves. So a run over many signals stays attributable event by event, and a run per signal is not needed to tell which one fired.\n\nUsually one key. Several when several of your signals matched the same event, which Trayo records on the one event rather than duplicating it. `[]` only where no signal is linked at all: an event from before these links were recorded, or one whose signal has since been deleted.\n\n`expand=signals` carries the same keys with each match's `type`, `confidence` and `evidence`; this field is the keys alone, and it costs no expansion."},"jobChange":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"type":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How the person moved — for example `new_hire` or `promotion`."},"date":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"When the move happened, as discovery read it off the profile."},"previousRole":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"company":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"since":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["title","company","since"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where they came from. `null` when discovery found no prior role."}},"required":["type","date","previousRole"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The move behind a `job_change` event — the same object webhook payloads carry as `jobChange`.\n\n`null` on every event whose `signalType` is not `job_change`. On a `job_change` event the object is always present, and its own fields read `null` where discovery could not classify the move."},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The only two states this API publishes. An event that has not reached one of them is not addressable yet — it 404s.\n\n`dismissed` means Trayo withdrew an event it had produced. To dismiss one yourself send `DELETE /v1/events/{id}`; the event then reads back in neither state."},"dismissalReason":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why Trayo withdrew this event — a short machine-readable tag such as `stale_job_change`, `no_signal_match` or `within_batch_duplicate:{eventId}`. The vocabulary is Trayo's own and grows over time, so read it as a label, not an enum.\n\nOnly ever set on an event whose `state` is `dismissed`, and only when a reason was recorded. `null` on every `live` event."},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"people":{"description":"Present only when `expand` was sent on the list route. Always present on `GET /v1/events/{id}`.\n\n**It is EMPTY until three preconditions hold, and an empty array here does not mean nobody was relevant.** Attaching people to an EVENT is a stage of its own. It runs only when (1) the account is ready — the same readiness `blockedSignals` reports on a discovery run, which the account reaches on its own shortly after it is created — (2) the workspace has a stakeholder definition, and (3) a discovery run you start yourself was started with `people: \"add\"`. The default, `people: \"none\"`, adds no people. A `job_change` event still names the person who changed jobs; add them with `POST /v1/people` if you want them.\n\n(2) and (3) are yours: `PATCH /v1/workspace`, and `people: \"add\"` on the run. All must be in place BEFORE the discovery run that produces the event — none is applied retroactively — so a workspace that sets its definition after its first runs gets `[]` on the events those runs produced and people on the ones after. Set it first.\n\nThe people listed here are already in your workspace: `id` is a person id you can pass to list and contact tools without adding them again. Importing an account adds no people; to add people at an account yourself, search with `POST /v1/find` and add the ones you choose with `POST /v1/people/batch`. `signals` expands normally.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why this person is relevant to this event."}},"required":["id","fullName","title","linkedinUrl","photoUrl","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}},"signals":{"description":"How well each of your signals matched, and from what reasoning. Same expansion rule as `people`. Send `expand` for that per-match detail, not to learn WHICH signals fired — every event names those in `signalKeys`, expanded or not.\n\nOne entry per stored match, ordered by `signalKey` like `signalKeys`, but NOT deduplicated the way that field is: a signal you have refined can hold two matches on one event, one per version of it. Such repeats carry IDENTICAL `confidence` and `evidence`, because the detail is looked up by `signalKey` — so a repeated key tells you nothing extra. Expect repeats and read `signalKeys` for the distinct set.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"signalKey":{"type":"string"},"type":{"type":"string"},"confidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["low","medium","high"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How sure the match was, on the three-step scale the matcher writes: `low`, `medium`, `high`. `null` when the run did not record one for this signal — older events, and signals attached by a stage that does not score its matches."},"evidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why this signal matched THIS event, in the matcher's own words — the sentence or two it reasoned from. `whyItMatters` argues the event to your workspace; this shows the work behind one signal.\n\n`null` when the run recorded no reasoning for this signal. Never an empty string."}},"required":["signalKey","type","confidence","evidence"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["id","accountId","accountName","accountUrl","title","summary","whyItMatters","offerings","url","publication","eventDate","signalType","signalKeys","jobChange","state","dismissalReason","createdAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"PersonListDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"data":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening."},"note":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.\n\n`null` when you have never set one. It reads back exactly as you wrote it — Trayo does not normalize, trim or rewrite it, and nothing in the product will ever change it."},"email":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use. A value you wrote (here or in the app) always wins; otherwise the one `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich` found; otherwise null. `emailSource` says which. A found email that later bounced is withheld — see `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for the reason."},"emailSource":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written by a caller or in the app; `enrichment` = found by a lookup."},"phone":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The phone number a lookup found, or null. Phones are only ever FOUND — there is no way to write one — so this is always a found value. Ask for one with `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich`, or with `trayo_enrich_phones` on the MCP server."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"City, e.g. `San Diego`. Null when the profile names only a region or metro area."},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State, province or region, e.g. `California`."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, e.g. `US`."},"raw":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The location as written on the profile, e.g. `Greater San Diego Area`. Usually present; the only populated field when the profile names just a metro area."}},"required":["city","state","country","raw"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where the person says they are on their LinkedIn profile. Usually their home metro area, but it is the person's own statement, not a verified address, and it is not where their company is headquartered. Null when Trayo has no location for them."},"profileImageUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The stored photo value, as written. Render `photoUrl` instead: it is the same photo under the name every person-returning route uses, with placeholders removed."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"isRepVisible":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether Trayo shows this person to a sales rep. Derived by the system and not settable — you cannot send this field, and a value you do send is ignored.\n\nA person is rep-visible if ANY of these holds: they are linked to a completed event; Trayo attached them to an account; or somebody deliberately added them, which is what `POST /v1/people` is. **So a person you create here comes back `true`** — creating a person through this API is treated as the same deliberate act as adding one by hand in the app, and it surfaces the same way.\n\nWhat it is NOT: it is not \"has an email\", and it is not a quality or confidence score. Adding an `email`, a `linkedinUsername` or a `title` neither sets it nor changes it.\n\n`false` is what you will see on people Trayo collected while searching but never surfaced — profiles no completed event and nothing else ever picked up. Filter them with `GET /v1/people?isRepVisible=false`; adding one with `POST /v1/people` turns it `true` on the same record."},"lastEventAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"stakeholderReport":{"description":"WHY THIS PERSON MATTERS — the stakeholder report Trayo generates for them: who they are, what they own, and how they relate to what you sell. Markdown, the same report the Trayo app shows on the person, and the same text `POST /v1/people/{id}/ask` reasons over.\n\n`null` until stakeholder research has run for this person — it is not produced on create, and a person you added through this API has none until it does. Never an empty string: a blank report reads as `null`, so a caller can test `=== null` and never has to trim.\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent. Without it the KEY is absent, which is not the same as `null`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"events":{"description":"The events this person is attached to and WHY, most recent `eventDate` first. `stakeholderReport` says why they matter to your workspace at all; this says why they were named on a particular thing that happened. It is the inverse of `people[].reasoning` on `GET /v1/events` — the same links, read from the person.\n\nOnly events that are themselves public are listed: an event still being processed, or one that was deleted, is not addressable on `GET /v1/events/{id}` and does not appear here either.\n\nAt most 50 links are listed, the most recent; a person attached to more than that is unusual, and the rest stay reachable through `GET /v1/events?accountId=...` with `expand=people`.\n\n`[]` is normal and does not mean nobody thought this person relevant: Trayo links people to an event only once it holds a company profile for the account AND your workspace has a stakeholder definition, and a discovery run you start adds them only with `people: \"add\"`. See `people` on `GET /v1/events` (`trayo_list_events` on the MCP server).\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"Read the full event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The same two states `GET /v1/events` publishes. `live` is what stands; `dismissed` is an event Trayo produced and then withdrew — the link to this person survives it, so it is listed rather than hidden and this field is how you tell the two apart."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why THIS person was named on THIS event, written when Trayo attached them. The same text `GET /v1/events` publishes as `people[].reasoning`, read from the other side."}},"required":["id","title","eventDate","signalType","state","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["id","accountId","fullName","title","note","email","emailSource","phone","linkedinUsername","linkedinUrl","location","profileImageUrl","photoUrl","isRepVisible","lastEventAt","createdAt","updatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"People, most recently created first."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"Another page exists. When `false` you have reached the end — stop requesting pages."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Pass verbatim as the next request's `cursor` to fetch the next page. Null exactly when `hasMore` is false."}},"required":["data","hasMore","nextCursor"],"additionalProperties":false},"CreatePersonDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"fullName":{"type":"string","maxLength":200},"title":{"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening.","type":"string","maxLength":200},"note":{"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.","type":"string","maxLength":1000},"email":{"type":"string","format":"email","pattern":"^(?!\\.)(?!.*\\.\\.)([A-Za-z0-9_'+\\-\\.]*)[A-Za-z0-9_+-]@([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9\\-]*\\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}$"},"linkedinUsername":{"type":"string","maxLength":200},"accountId":{"description":"Link to an existing account. MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with `company` — sending both is `validation_failed` (`param: \"company\"`). An id that does not resolve in your workspace is `unknown_account`.\n\nSend NEITHER this nor `company` and the person is linked to the employer on their LinkedIn profile when the row carries a LinkedIn identity and Trayo has one on file — creating that account if it is new — and is left unlinked otherwise. Send one of the two to decide the account yourself.","type":"string","pattern":"^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$","format":"uuid"},"company":{"description":"Link by company name instead of id, creating the account if needed. Mutually exclusive with `accountId`. See this object's own description — `domain` is optional, and a company you do not already have is created with an empty `url` rather than refused when you omit it.","type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string","maxLength":200,"description":"Matched case-insensitively against your existing accounts. A match reuses that account and `domain` is ignored."},"domain":{"description":"Seeds the new account's `url` when no existing account matches `name`. Omit it freely when linking to a company you already have, or when you have no website at all: the account is then created with no `url`. A bare host (`acme.com`) or a full website URL (`https://acme.com/about`) are both fine: the scheme, `www.`, path and query are dropped and the account is created at `https://acme.com`. A value that is not a usable hostname is refused — `validation_failed`, `param: \"company.domain\"`.","type":"string","maxLength":255}},"required":["name"],"additionalProperties":false},"photoUrl":{"description":"The person's profile image URL. For a new person, omit it on a row that carries a LinkedIn identity and Trayo fills it from the profile it has on file, self-hosting the image; a value you send is kept as-is.","type":"string","maxLength":2000},"id":{"description":"Accepted and IGNORED — declared only so a `POST /v1/find` contacts result can be posted here verbatim. Never persisted or echoed back.","type":"string","maxLength":200},"linkedinUrl":{"description":"A LinkedIn PROFILE URL (`https://www.linkedin.com/in/ada-lovelace`), accepted so a `POST /v1/find` contacts result can be posted here verbatim.\n\n**It is USED, not ignored:** when `linkedinUsername` is absent, the username is taken from this URL and stored. `linkedinUsername` WINS when both are present and they disagree — this field never overwrites one you sent.\n\nCountry subdomains work (`https://il.linkedin.com/in/ada-lovelace`), as does a bare username. A LinkedIn URL that is not a personal profile — a `/company/` page, the feed — yields no username and is IGNORED rather than rejected, so one such row cannot fail an otherwise good import; the person is created with `linkedinUsername: null`.\n\nThe URL itself is not stored and does not read back: `linkedinUsername` is the field this API keeps, and it is what `GET /v1/people` returns.\n\n**For a new person with no strong-key match, it also FILLS the fields you left empty.** Sending a LinkedIn identity and no `title`, no `photoUrl`, or neither an `accountId` nor a `company` makes Trayo look the person up in its own profile mirror and use what it finds for exactly those fields — so the response can carry a `title`, a `profileImageUrl` and an `accountId` you never sent, and a company Trayo has never seen becomes a NEW account (with an empty `url`, like any name-only company). It never overwrites a value you did send, and it is best-effort: a miss, or a lookup that takes too long, creates the new row exactly as you sent it rather than failing it. There is no way to opt out other than by supplying the fields yourself.","type":"string","maxLength":500},"state":{"description":"Accepted and IGNORED — declared only so a `POST /v1/find` contacts result can be posted here verbatim. Never persisted or echoed back.","type":"string","maxLength":40}},"required":["fullName"],"description":"**Unknown fields are rejected** (`validation_failed`), not silently dropped. The fields marked \"accepted and ignored\" are the remaining members of a `POST /v1/find` contacts result, declared so a find result can be posted here verbatim.","additionalProperties":false},"PersonDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening."},"note":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.\n\n`null` when you have never set one. It reads back exactly as you wrote it — Trayo does not normalize, trim or rewrite it, and nothing in the product will ever change it."},"email":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use. A value you wrote (here or in the app) always wins; otherwise the one `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich` found; otherwise null. `emailSource` says which. A found email that later bounced is withheld — see `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for the reason."},"emailSource":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written by a caller or in the app; `enrichment` = found by a lookup."},"phone":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The phone number a lookup found, or null. Phones are only ever FOUND — there is no way to write one — so this is always a found value. Ask for one with `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich`, or with `trayo_enrich_phones` on the MCP server."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"City, e.g. `San Diego`. Null when the profile names only a region or metro area."},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State, province or region, e.g. `California`."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, e.g. `US`."},"raw":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The location as written on the profile, e.g. `Greater San Diego Area`. Usually present; the only populated field when the profile names just a metro area."}},"required":["city","state","country","raw"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where the person says they are on their LinkedIn profile. Usually their home metro area, but it is the person's own statement, not a verified address, and it is not where their company is headquartered. Null when Trayo has no location for them."},"profileImageUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The stored photo value, as written. Render `photoUrl` instead: it is the same photo under the name every person-returning route uses, with placeholders removed."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"isRepVisible":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether Trayo shows this person to a sales rep. Derived by the system and not settable — you cannot send this field, and a value you do send is ignored.\n\nA person is rep-visible if ANY of these holds: they are linked to a completed event; Trayo attached them to an account; or somebody deliberately added them, which is what `POST /v1/people` is. **So a person you create here comes back `true`** — creating a person through this API is treated as the same deliberate act as adding one by hand in the app, and it surfaces the same way.\n\nWhat it is NOT: it is not \"has an email\", and it is not a quality or confidence score. Adding an `email`, a `linkedinUsername` or a `title` neither sets it nor changes it.\n\n`false` is what you will see on people Trayo collected while searching but never surfaced — profiles no completed event and nothing else ever picked up. Filter them with `GET /v1/people?isRepVisible=false`; adding one with `POST /v1/people` turns it `true` on the same record."},"lastEventAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"stakeholderReport":{"description":"WHY THIS PERSON MATTERS — the stakeholder report Trayo generates for them: who they are, what they own, and how they relate to what you sell. Markdown, the same report the Trayo app shows on the person, and the same text `POST /v1/people/{id}/ask` reasons over.\n\n`null` until stakeholder research has run for this person — it is not produced on create, and a person you added through this API has none until it does. Never an empty string: a blank report reads as `null`, so a caller can test `=== null` and never has to trim.\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent. Without it the KEY is absent, which is not the same as `null`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"events":{"description":"The events this person is attached to and WHY, most recent `eventDate` first. `stakeholderReport` says why they matter to your workspace at all; this says why they were named on a particular thing that happened. It is the inverse of `people[].reasoning` on `GET /v1/events` — the same links, read from the person.\n\nOnly events that are themselves public are listed: an event still being processed, or one that was deleted, is not addressable on `GET /v1/events/{id}` and does not appear here either.\n\nAt most 50 links are listed, the most recent; a person attached to more than that is unusual, and the rest stay reachable through `GET /v1/events?accountId=...` with `expand=people`.\n\n`[]` is normal and does not mean nobody thought this person relevant: Trayo links people to an event only once it holds a company profile for the account AND your workspace has a stakeholder definition, and a discovery run you start adds them only with `people: \"add\"`. See `people` on `GET /v1/events` (`trayo_list_events` on the MCP server).\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"Read the full event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The same two states `GET /v1/events` publishes. `live` is what stands; `dismissed` is an event Trayo produced and then withdrew — the link to this person survives it, so it is listed rather than hidden and this field is how you tell the two apart."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why THIS person was named on THIS event, written when Trayo attached them. The same text `GET /v1/events` publishes as `people[].reasoning`, read from the other side."}},"required":["id","title","eventDate","signalType","state","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["id","accountId","fullName","title","note","email","emailSource","phone","linkedinUsername","linkedinUrl","location","profileImageUrl","photoUrl","isRepVisible","lastEventAt","createdAt","updatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"BatchCreatePeopleDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"people":{"maxItems":200,"type":"array","items":{},"description":"Up to 200 rows, each shaped like a `POST /v1/people` body. More than 200 is a `validation_failed` ERROR (`param: \"people\"`), never a silent truncation — the deliberate same rule as `POST /v1/find`'s `limit`, which is rejected above 100.\n\nA row whose `accountId`/`company` cannot be resolved is reported in `skipped`, not raised as a request error."}},"required":["people"],"additionalProperties":false},"BatchCreatePeopleResultDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"created":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening."},"note":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.\n\n`null` when you have never set one. It reads back exactly as you wrote it — Trayo does not normalize, trim or rewrite it, and nothing in the product will ever change it."},"email":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use. A value you wrote (here or in the app) always wins; otherwise the one `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich` found; otherwise null. `emailSource` says which. A found email that later bounced is withheld — see `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for the reason."},"emailSource":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written by a caller or in the app; `enrichment` = found by a lookup."},"phone":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The phone number a lookup found, or null. Phones are only ever FOUND — there is no way to write one — so this is always a found value. Ask for one with `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich`, or with `trayo_enrich_phones` on the MCP server."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"City, e.g. `San Diego`. Null when the profile names only a region or metro area."},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State, province or region, e.g. `California`."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, e.g. `US`."},"raw":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The location as written on the profile, e.g. `Greater San Diego Area`. Usually present; the only populated field when the profile names just a metro area."}},"required":["city","state","country","raw"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where the person says they are on their LinkedIn profile. Usually their home metro area, but it is the person's own statement, not a verified address, and it is not where their company is headquartered. Null when Trayo has no location for them."},"profileImageUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The stored photo value, as written. Render `photoUrl` instead: it is the same photo under the name every person-returning route uses, with placeholders removed."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"isRepVisible":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether Trayo shows this person to a sales rep. Derived by the system and not settable — you cannot send this field, and a value you do send is ignored.\n\nA person is rep-visible if ANY of these holds: they are linked to a completed event; Trayo attached them to an account; or somebody deliberately added them, which is what `POST /v1/people` is. **So a person you create here comes back `true`** — creating a person through this API is treated as the same deliberate act as adding one by hand in the app, and it surfaces the same way.\n\nWhat it is NOT: it is not \"has an email\", and it is not a quality or confidence score. Adding an `email`, a `linkedinUsername` or a `title` neither sets it nor changes it.\n\n`false` is what you will see on people Trayo collected while searching but never surfaced — profiles no completed event and nothing else ever picked up. Filter them with `GET /v1/people?isRepVisible=false`; adding one with `POST /v1/people` turns it `true` on the same record."},"lastEventAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"stakeholderReport":{"description":"WHY THIS PERSON MATTERS — the stakeholder report Trayo generates for them: who they are, what they own, and how they relate to what you sell. Markdown, the same report the Trayo app shows on the person, and the same text `POST /v1/people/{id}/ask` reasons over.\n\n`null` until stakeholder research has run for this person — it is not produced on create, and a person you added through this API has none until it does. Never an empty string: a blank report reads as `null`, so a caller can test `=== null` and never has to trim.\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent. Without it the KEY is absent, which is not the same as `null`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"events":{"description":"The events this person is attached to and WHY, most recent `eventDate` first. `stakeholderReport` says why they matter to your workspace at all; this says why they were named on a particular thing that happened. It is the inverse of `people[].reasoning` on `GET /v1/events` — the same links, read from the person.\n\nOnly events that are themselves public are listed: an event still being processed, or one that was deleted, is not addressable on `GET /v1/events/{id}` and does not appear here either.\n\nAt most 50 links are listed, the most recent; a person attached to more than that is unusual, and the rest stay reachable through `GET /v1/events?accountId=...` with `expand=people`.\n\n`[]` is normal and does not mean nobody thought this person relevant: Trayo links people to an event only once it holds a company profile for the account AND your workspace has a stakeholder definition, and a discovery run you start adds them only with `people: \"add\"`. See `people` on `GET /v1/events` (`trayo_list_events` on the MCP server).\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"Read the full event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The same two states `GET /v1/events` publishes. `live` is what stands; `dismissed` is an event Trayo produced and then withdrew — the link to this person survives it, so it is listed rather than hidden and this field is how you tell the two apart."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why THIS person was named on THIS event, written when Trayo attached them. The same text `GET /v1/events` publishes as `people[].reasoning`, read from the other side."}},"required":["id","title","eventDate","signalType","state","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["id","accountId","fullName","title","note","email","emailSource","phone","linkedinUsername","linkedinUrl","location","profileImageUrl","photoUrl","isRepVisible","lastEventAt","createdAt","updatedAt"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The rows that were created, in submission order."},"skipped":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"0-based position of the offending row in the submitted array."},"reason":{"type":"string","description":"Why this row was not created, in the same wording the single-create route would have returned for the same failure. PROSE — do not branch on it; branch on `reasonCode`."},"reasonCode":{"description":"The machine-readable class of the skip, present only for the classes a caller has to act on differently. `duplicate` means the row collided with a record you already have (or with an earlier row in the same batch) — `existingId` names it, and the remedy is a `PATCH`, not a re-post. `identity_conflict` means the submitted LinkedIn identity and email identify two different people; send one identity, or PATCH the intended record explicitly. Every other skip is a problem with the row itself: this field is ABSENT and `reason` says what to fix.","type":"string","enum":["duplicate","identity_conflict"]},"existingId":{"description":"The record this row collided with. Present exactly when `reasonCode` is `duplicate`, so a caller can `PATCH` the existing record instead of guessing which of its rows already existed. It may name a row created EARLIER IN THIS SAME batch.","type":"string","format":"uuid"}},"required":["index","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Rows that were NOT created. Always inspect this: a `200` means the call succeeded, not that every row landed."},"degraded":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"0-based position of this row in the submitted array."},"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"The id of the created row this entry describes."},"codes":{"minItems":1,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["no_account","no_identity"]},"description":"One or both, since they are independent conditions. `no_account` = created with no `accountId`. `no_identity` = created with neither a LinkedIn username nor an email. Whether a replay recreates it depends on the account: with none there is nothing left to match on, so it is created again; inside an account `fullName` is still a dedup key, so the replay is skipped as a duplicate. The `reason` says which case this row is."},"reason":{"type":"string","description":"Prose explanation, for a human. Branch on `codes`, not on this."}},"required":["index","id","codes","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Rows that WERE created (they also appear in `created`) but landed without an account and/or without a LinkedIn/email identity. Always inspect this alongside `created`: a row here is not an error, but it is not a clean import either."},"updated":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"format":"uuid"},"fullName":{"type":"string"},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening."},"note":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.\n\n`null` when you have never set one. It reads back exactly as you wrote it — Trayo does not normalize, trim or rewrite it, and nothing in the product will ever change it."},"email":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use. A value you wrote (here or in the app) always wins; otherwise the one `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich` found; otherwise null. `emailSource` says which. A found email that later bounced is withheld — see `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for the reason."},"emailSource":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written by a caller or in the app; `enrichment` = found by a lookup."},"phone":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The phone number a lookup found, or null. Phones are only ever FOUND — there is no way to write one — so this is always a found value. Ask for one with `POST /v1/people/{id}/enrich`, or with `trayo_enrich_phones` on the MCP server."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile URL, when Trayo has it. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"City, e.g. `San Diego`. Null when the profile names only a region or metro area."},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State, province or region, e.g. `California`."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, e.g. `US`."},"raw":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The location as written on the profile, e.g. `Greater San Diego Area`. Usually present; the only populated field when the profile names just a metro area."}},"required":["city","state","country","raw"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where the person says they are on their LinkedIn profile. Usually their home metro area, but it is the person's own statement, not a verified address, and it is not where their company is headquartered. Null when Trayo has no location for them."},"profileImageUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The stored photo value, as written. Render `photoUrl` instead: it is the same photo under the name every person-returning route uses, with placeholders removed."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"isRepVisible":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether Trayo shows this person to a sales rep. Derived by the system and not settable — you cannot send this field, and a value you do send is ignored.\n\nA person is rep-visible if ANY of these holds: they are linked to a completed event; Trayo attached them to an account; or somebody deliberately added them, which is what `POST /v1/people` is. **So a person you create here comes back `true`** — creating a person through this API is treated as the same deliberate act as adding one by hand in the app, and it surfaces the same way.\n\nWhat it is NOT: it is not \"has an email\", and it is not a quality or confidence score. Adding an `email`, a `linkedinUsername` or a `title` neither sets it nor changes it.\n\n`false` is what you will see on people Trayo collected while searching but never surfaced — profiles no completed event and nothing else ever picked up. Filter them with `GET /v1/people?isRepVisible=false`; adding one with `POST /v1/people` turns it `true` on the same record."},"lastEventAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"createdAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"updatedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"stakeholderReport":{"description":"WHY THIS PERSON MATTERS — the stakeholder report Trayo generates for them: who they are, what they own, and how they relate to what you sell. Markdown, the same report the Trayo app shows on the person, and the same text `POST /v1/people/{id}/ask` reasons over.\n\n`null` until stakeholder research has run for this person — it is not produced on create, and a person you added through this API has none until it does. Never an empty string: a blank report reads as `null`, so a caller can test `=== null` and never has to trim.\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent. Without it the KEY is absent, which is not the same as `null`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"events":{"description":"The events this person is attached to and WHY, most recent `eventDate` first. `stakeholderReport` says why they matter to your workspace at all; this says why they were named on a particular thing that happened. It is the inverse of `people[].reasoning` on `GET /v1/events` — the same links, read from the person.\n\nOnly events that are themselves public are listed: an event still being processed, or one that was deleted, is not addressable on `GET /v1/events/{id}` and does not appear here either.\n\nAt most 50 links are listed, the most recent; a person attached to more than that is unusual, and the rest stay reachable through `GET /v1/events?accountId=...` with `expand=people`.\n\n`[]` is normal and does not mean nobody thought this person relevant: Trayo links people to an event only once it holds a company profile for the account AND your workspace has a stakeholder definition, and a discovery run you start adds them only with `people: \"add\"`. See `people` on `GET /v1/events` (`trayo_list_events` on the MCP server).\n\nPresent on `GET /v1/people/{id}`, and on `GET /v1/people` only when `expand` is sent.","type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"Read the full event with `GET /v1/events/{id}`."},"title":{"type":"string"},"eventDate":{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},"signalType":{"type":"string"},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["live","dismissed"],"description":"The same two states `GET /v1/events` publishes. `live` is what stands; `dismissed` is an event Trayo produced and then withdrew — the link to this person survives it, so it is listed rather than hidden and this field is how you tell the two apart."},"reasoning":{"type":"string","description":"Why THIS person was named on THIS event, written when Trayo attached them. The same text `GET /v1/events` publishes as `people[].reasoning`, read from the other side."}},"required":["id","title","eventDate","signalType","state","reasoning"],"additionalProperties":false}},"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"0-based position of the row in the submitted array."}},"required":["id","accountId","fullName","title","note","email","emailSource","phone","linkedinUsername","linkedinUrl","location","profileImageUrl","photoUrl","isRepVisible","lastEventAt","createdAt","updatedAt","index"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Existing people updated by a LinkedIn or email match, in submission order. `index` maps each record back to the submitted row. These rows do not also appear in `created`."}},"required":["created","skipped","degraded","updated"],"additionalProperties":false},"UpdatePersonDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"fullName":{"type":"string","maxLength":200},"title":{"description":"The person's JOB TITLE — nothing else. It is a published field: it is shown to humans as a job title and read as one by everything downstream, so a value like `\"VP Sales (UNVERIFIED, sourced from LinkedIn)\"` is a wrong job title for every reader of it, not an annotated one.\n\n**Sourcing, confidence, dates and \"unverified\" go in `note`**, which exists for exactly that, holds 1000 characters, and is never interpreted by Trayo.\n\nOmit it and Trayo may fill it for you: a row carrying a LinkedIn identity and no title takes one from the profile Trayo has on file — the current role's title, or the profile headline when there is no current role. Send the field to keep that from happening.","type":"string","maxLength":200},"note":{"description":"Free text that is YOURS. Trayo stores it, returns it, and never reads it — it is not parsed, not indexed, not searchable, not shown in the Trayo UI, and nothing in the product branches on it. No structure is imposed: `\"sourced from LinkedIn, unverified, 2026-09-02\"` is exactly the intended use.\n\nYours, not PRIVATE: it is stored on the person record, so anyone with access to your workspace can read it back. Do not put secrets in it.\n\n**This is where provenance, sourcing, confidence and \"unverified\" go.** Putting them in `title` instead corrupts a published field that humans and downstream systems read as a job title.\n\nUp to 1000 characters; longer is `validation_failed`, not a truncation.\n\nSend `null` to CLEAR the note; omit the field to leave whatever is there untouched. Sending a string replaces the whole note — there is no append.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","maxLength":1000},{"type":"null"}]},"email":{"type":"string","format":"email","pattern":"^(?!\\.)(?!.*\\.\\.)([A-Za-z0-9_'+\\-\\.]*)[A-Za-z0-9_+-]@([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9\\-]*\\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}$"},"linkedinUsername":{"type":"string","maxLength":200}},"description":"Every field is optional; only fields present are changed. `accountId` and `company` are absent on purpose — an update cannot move a person between accounts.","additionalProperties":false},"BatchEnrichBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"personIds":{"minItems":1,"maxItems":200,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$","format":"uuid"},"description":"1 to 200 person ids. Over the cap is an error, not a clamp."},"fields":{"description":"Which values to look up. Omit for both. A field that cannot or need not run is reported in `skipped`, never an error.","minItems":1,"maxItems":2,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]}}},"required":["personIds"],"additionalProperties":false},"BatchEnrichResultDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"accepted":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"started":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]},"description":"The lookups that were started by THIS call. Poll `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for them, or `trayo_get_contacts` on the MCP server."},"skipped":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"field":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["manual_present","already_found","in_progress","retry_too_soon","start_failed"],"description":"`manual_present`: you wrote an email, it wins, nothing to find. `already_found`: a value is already on the record. `in_progress`: a lookup is already running. `retry_too_soon`: the last run missed and the cooldown has not passed — `retryAvailableAt` says when. `start_failed`: this field passed every check but could not be started. Nothing is running for it, and re-sending is safe and is not charged twice. (When NO field could be started the call is a `503 lookup_start_failed` instead.)"},"retryAvailableAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["field","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The lookups this call did NOT start, and why. Never an error."},"contact":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"email":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use: a manual value if one was written, else the found one."},"source":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written through this API or the app; `enrichment` = found."},"status":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["verified","unconfirmed","bounced","stale"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State of a FOUND email, and the field to act on. `verified` = a finder returned it and nothing suggests it belongs to someone else. `unconfirmed` = a finder returned it, but `identityConfidence` is `conflict`: the address disagrees with this person's name or company, or another person in the workspace holds the same address. The value is still given; have a human check it before sending. `bounced` after a send bounced (the value is withheld); `stale` when the product no longer trusts it. Neither `verified` nor `unconfirmed` is a mailbox probe. Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"identityConfidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["match","conflict","unknown"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Whether a FOUND email looks like THIS person's: `match` when its local-part agrees with their name or its domain with their account's website; `conflict` when either disagrees (a catch-all or a wrong-account hit) or when another person in the workspace holds the same address, and `status` then reads `unconfirmed`; `unknown` when there is nothing to compare against (no name, no account website, a free-mail domain, a sibling domain of the account's, or an account whose website matches none of its people's addresses). Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","source","status","identityConfidence","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false},"phone":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The found phone number, or null."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["personId","email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The state immediately after those lookups started."}},"required":["personId","started","skipped","contact"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"One outcome per person that exists in your workspace, in submission order."},"skipped":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"0-based position in the submitted `personIds`."},"reason":{"type":"string"}},"required":["index","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Person ids this call could not act on at all: unknown or another workspace's, a duplicate earlier in the batch, a plan limit that stopped the batch, or the time limit the call reached before it got to them — the reason says which. Rows after a limit are skipped, not partially run; rows the call ran out of time for were never started and never charged, so send them again."},"stoppedBy":{"description":"Present when a workspace allowance ended the batch before every person was evaluated. Branch on this rather than on `skipped[].reason` prose.","type":"object","properties":{"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["lookup_limit_reached","quota_daily_limit","quota_exceeded"],"description":"Which workspace allowance ended the batch. `lookup_limit_reached`: the plan's lifetime email/phone lookup allowance is used up — it does not reset, the plan has to change. `quota_exceeded`: the workspace quota — raise it or wait for it to renew. `quota_daily_limit`: the rolling 24-hour usage limit — wait, then retry."},"retry":{"type":"string","enum":["retry_later","do_not_retry"],"description":"Whether waiting alone clears this stop — the same `retry` an error envelope for this code carries. `do_not_retry` means re-sending the skipped people changes nothing until the allowance itself changes."},"processed":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many submitted people this call evaluated before the stop (they are in `accepted`)."},"skippedOther":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many submitted people before the stop were skipped for their own reason — an unknown id or a duplicate. They are in `skipped`; fix and re-send them. `processed + skippedOther + notLookedUp` is the number of people submitted."},"notLookedUp":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"How many submitted people the allowance left unevaluated — the person it refused plus everyone after them. They are in `skipped`; nothing was started or charged for them."},"message":{"type":"string","description":"The refusal, in words, with what to do next."}},"required":["reason","retry","processed","skippedOther","notLookedUp","message"],"additionalProperties":false},"quota":{"type":"object","properties":{"email":{"type":"object","properties":{"enabled":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the plan allows this kind of lookup at all. `false` means every lookup is refused."},"limit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Effective allowance for this field. `null` means unlimited. Public REST/MCP lookup quotas, workspace quota and the daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`, a rolling 24-hour ceiling) are disabled for now. Scope, lifecycle and request validation still apply."},"used":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Recorded lookup usage, including lookups still in flight, even when no cap applies. Read `remaining` rather than subtracting this from `limit`."},"remaining":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Lookups left against an enabled allowance: `limit - used`, floored at 0. `null` when `limit` is `null`; public REST/MCP usage allowances are unlimited for now. The rolling 24-hour daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`) is disabled too. This is a snapshot that includes lookups still in flight; concurrent work changes `used`. Scope, lifecycle, per-network protection and row validation can still refuse a call. Handle the actual refusal code and, for a stopped MCP batch, `stoppedBy.retry`."},"period":{"type":"string","const":"lifetime","description":"The usage counting period. `lifetime`: recorded lookups do not reset. When a numeric allowance applies, it has no scheduled reset; `null` limits remain uncapped."}},"required":["enabled","limit","used","remaining","period"],"additionalProperties":false},"phone":{"type":"object","properties":{"enabled":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the plan allows this kind of lookup at all. `false` means every lookup is refused."},"limit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Effective allowance for this field. `null` means unlimited. Public REST/MCP lookup quotas, workspace quota and the daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`, a rolling 24-hour ceiling) are disabled for now. Scope, lifecycle and request validation still apply."},"used":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Recorded lookup usage, including lookups still in flight, even when no cap applies. Read `remaining` rather than subtracting this from `limit`."},"remaining":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Lookups left against an enabled allowance: `limit - used`, floored at 0. `null` when `limit` is `null`; public REST/MCP usage allowances are unlimited for now. The rolling 24-hour daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`) is disabled too. This is a snapshot that includes lookups still in flight; concurrent work changes `used`. Scope, lifecycle, per-network protection and row validation can still refuse a call. Handle the actual refusal code and, for a stopped MCP batch, `stoppedBy.retry`."},"period":{"type":"string","const":"lifetime","description":"The usage counting period. `lifetime`: recorded lookups do not reset. When a numeric allowance applies, it has no scheduled reset; `null` limits remain uncapped."}},"required":["enabled","limit","used","remaining","period"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Email and phone lookup usage and any active allowances AFTER this call, counted separately. `limit` and `remaining` are `null` when no lookup cap applies. If `remaining` is a number, size your next request by it: people past that allowance are skipped. Omitted only when the allowance could not be read; `GET /v1/whoami` (MCP: trayo_whoami) reports the same numbers."}},"required":["accepted","skipped"],"description":"**Partial success is the contract.** A person whose fields were all skipped still lands in `accepted`, with the reasons; only a person the call could not evaluate lands in `skipped`.","additionalProperties":false},"EnrichPersonBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"fields":{"description":"Which values to look up. Omit for both. A field that cannot or need not run is reported in `skipped`, never an error.","minItems":1,"maxItems":2,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]}}},"additionalProperties":false},"EnrichPersonResultDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"started":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]},"description":"The lookups that were started by THIS call. Poll `GET /v1/people/{id}/enrich` for them, or `trayo_get_contacts` on the MCP server."},"skipped":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"field":{"type":"string","enum":["email","phone"]},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["manual_present","already_found","in_progress","retry_too_soon","start_failed"],"description":"`manual_present`: you wrote an email, it wins, nothing to find. `already_found`: a value is already on the record. `in_progress`: a lookup is already running. `retry_too_soon`: the last run missed and the cooldown has not passed — `retryAvailableAt` says when. `start_failed`: this field passed every check but could not be started. Nothing is running for it, and re-sending is safe and is not charged twice. (When NO field could be started the call is a `503 lookup_start_failed` instead.)"},"retryAvailableAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["field","reason"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The lookups this call did NOT start, and why. Never an error."},"contact":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"email":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use: a manual value if one was written, else the found one."},"source":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written through this API or the app; `enrichment` = found."},"status":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["verified","unconfirmed","bounced","stale"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State of a FOUND email, and the field to act on. `verified` = a finder returned it and nothing suggests it belongs to someone else. `unconfirmed` = a finder returned it, but `identityConfidence` is `conflict`: the address disagrees with this person's name or company, or another person in the workspace holds the same address. The value is still given; have a human check it before sending. `bounced` after a send bounced (the value is withheld); `stale` when the product no longer trusts it. Neither `verified` nor `unconfirmed` is a mailbox probe. Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"identityConfidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["match","conflict","unknown"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Whether a FOUND email looks like THIS person's: `match` when its local-part agrees with their name or its domain with their account's website; `conflict` when either disagrees (a catch-all or a wrong-account hit) or when another person in the workspace holds the same address, and `status` then reads `unconfirmed`; `unknown` when there is nothing to compare against (no name, no account website, a free-mail domain, a sibling domain of the account's, or an account whose website matches none of its people's addresses). Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","source","status","identityConfidence","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false},"phone":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The found phone number, or null."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["personId","email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The state immediately after those lookups started."},"quota":{"type":"object","properties":{"email":{"type":"object","properties":{"enabled":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the plan allows this kind of lookup at all. `false` means every lookup is refused."},"limit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Effective allowance for this field. `null` means unlimited. Public REST/MCP lookup quotas, workspace quota and the daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`, a rolling 24-hour ceiling) are disabled for now. Scope, lifecycle and request validation still apply."},"used":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Recorded lookup usage, including lookups still in flight, even when no cap applies. Read `remaining` rather than subtracting this from `limit`."},"remaining":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Lookups left against an enabled allowance: `limit - used`, floored at 0. `null` when `limit` is `null`; public REST/MCP usage allowances are unlimited for now. The rolling 24-hour daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`) is disabled too. This is a snapshot that includes lookups still in flight; concurrent work changes `used`. Scope, lifecycle, per-network protection and row validation can still refuse a call. Handle the actual refusal code and, for a stopped MCP batch, `stoppedBy.retry`."},"period":{"type":"string","const":"lifetime","description":"The usage counting period. `lifetime`: recorded lookups do not reset. When a numeric allowance applies, it has no scheduled reset; `null` limits remain uncapped."}},"required":["enabled","limit","used","remaining","period"],"additionalProperties":false},"phone":{"type":"object","properties":{"enabled":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the plan allows this kind of lookup at all. `false` means every lookup is refused."},"limit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Effective allowance for this field. `null` means unlimited. Public REST/MCP lookup quotas, workspace quota and the daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`, a rolling 24-hour ceiling) are disabled for now. Scope, lifecycle and request validation still apply."},"used":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Recorded lookup usage, including lookups still in flight, even when no cap applies. Read `remaining` rather than subtracting this from `limit`."},"remaining":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Lookups left against an enabled allowance: `limit - used`, floored at 0. `null` when `limit` is `null`; public REST/MCP usage allowances are unlimited for now. The rolling 24-hour daily usage limit (`429 quota_daily_limit`) is disabled too. This is a snapshot that includes lookups still in flight; concurrent work changes `used`. Scope, lifecycle, per-network protection and row validation can still refuse a call. Handle the actual refusal code and, for a stopped MCP batch, `stoppedBy.retry`."},"period":{"type":"string","const":"lifetime","description":"The usage counting period. `lifetime`: recorded lookups do not reset. When a numeric allowance applies, it has no scheduled reset; `null` limits remain uncapped."}},"required":["enabled","limit","used","remaining","period"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Email and phone lookup usage and any active allowances AFTER this call, counted separately. `limit` and `remaining` are `null` when no lookup cap applies. If `remaining` is a number, size your next request by it: people past that allowance are skipped. Omitted only when the allowance could not be read; `GET /v1/whoami` (MCP: trayo_whoami) reports the same numbers."}},"required":["personId","started","skipped","contact"],"additionalProperties":false},"ContactStatusDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},"email":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The email to use: a manual value if one was written, else the found one."},"source":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["manual","enrichment"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`manual` = written through this API or the app; `enrichment` = found."},"status":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["verified","unconfirmed","bounced","stale"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State of a FOUND email, and the field to act on. `verified` = a finder returned it and nothing suggests it belongs to someone else. `unconfirmed` = a finder returned it, but `identityConfidence` is `conflict`: the address disagrees with this person's name or company, or another person in the workspace holds the same address. The value is still given; have a human check it before sending. `bounced` after a send bounced (the value is withheld); `stale` when the product no longer trusts it. Neither `verified` nor `unconfirmed` is a mailbox probe. Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"identityConfidence":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["match","conflict","unknown"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Whether a FOUND email looks like THIS person's: `match` when its local-part agrees with their name or its domain with their account's website; `conflict` when either disagrees (a catch-all or a wrong-account hit) or when another person in the workspace holds the same address, and `status` then reads `unconfirmed`; `unknown` when there is nothing to compare against (no name, no account website, a free-mail domain, a sibling domain of the account's, or an account whose website matches none of its people's addresses). Null for a manual value and when nothing was found."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","source","status","identityConfidence","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false},"phone":{"type":"object","properties":{"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The found phone number, or null."},"foundAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]},"lookup":{"type":"object","properties":{"state":{"type":"string","enum":["not_requested","in_progress","found","not_found","error","bounced"],"description":"`not_requested`: never looked up. `in_progress`: a lookup is running, poll. `found`: a value is on the record. `not_found`: the lookup ran and missed; `retryAvailableAt` says when it may run again. `error`: a run stalled and may be retried now. `bounced`: a found email bounced; the next lookup re-runs from scratch."},"retryAvailableAt":{"description":"Set on `not_found`: the earliest time a new lookup will be accepted. Until then the field is skipped as `retry_too_soon`.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["state","retryAvailableAt"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["value","foundAt","lookup"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["personId","email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false},"AskPersonBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"question":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2000,"description":"The question, in plain prose. Anything a rep might ask a colleague who knows the person: \"Is she the right buyer for us?\", \"What have we sent him and did he respond?\", \"How long has she been in this role, and where was she before?\", \"Give me three talking points for a first call.\""},"intent":{"description":"What you will do with the answer — one or two sentences. It steers focus, depth and form. \"Qualifying whether to add them to a sequence; I need a yes/no with the reason\" gets a verdict; \"Drafting a first-touch email; I need specific hooks I can cite\" gets hooks with their sources; \"Briefing a rep before a call\" gets a short brief. Omit it for a direct, concise answer.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2000}},"required":["question"],"additionalProperties":false},"AskPersonResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"personId":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","description":"The person this answer is about — the id you sent in the path."},"question":{"type":"string","description":"Your question, as answered."},"intent":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Your intent, as applied; null when you sent none."},"answer":{"type":"string","description":"The answer, in Markdown. Grounded in the dossier only: a fact the dossier does not hold is named as unknown, never guessed."},"gaps":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"What was missing that would have made the answer better, up to five items — e.g. \"no LinkedIn profile linked\", \"no outreach on record\". Empty when nothing material was missing."},"sources":{"type":"object","properties":{"trayo":{"type":"object","properties":{"contact":{"type":"object","properties":{"email":{"type":"boolean","description":"An email is on record (written or found)."},"phone":{"type":"boolean","description":"A found phone is on record."}},"required":["email","phone"],"additionalProperties":false},"account":{"type":"boolean","description":"The person is linked to an account and its record was included."},"accountResearch":{"type":"boolean","description":"That account carries research Trayo has gathered for it, and it was included."},"events":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Signals (events) the person is attached to, included."},"sequences":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Sequences composed for the person, included."},"emailsSent":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Emails sent to the person, included."},"composedMessages":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"One-off messages composed and copied for the person, included."},"lists":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Lists the person is on, included."},"synthesis":{"type":"boolean","description":"A stored person synthesis exists and was included."},"stakeholderReport":{"type":"boolean","description":"A stored stakeholder report exists and was included."},"note":{"type":"boolean","description":"The `note` you wrote on the person was included."}},"required":["contact","account","accountResearch","events","sequences","emailsSent","composedMessages","lists","synthesis","stakeholderReport","note"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Which workspace records went into the prompt."},"linkedin":{"type":"object","properties":{"status":{"type":"string","enum":["found","not_found","not_linked","unavailable"],"description":"`found`: the profile was included. `not_found`: the person is linked to LinkedIn but Trayo has no record. `not_linked`: the person has no LinkedIn identity in Trayo — set `linkedinUsername` with `PATCH /v1/people/{id}`. `unavailable`: the data could not be reached this time; the answer used Trayo data only."},"matchedBy":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["stored_id","linkedin_username"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How the profile was found: `stored_id`, the id Trayo stored when it collected the person; `linkedin_username`, a lookup by the LinkedIn handle. Null unless `status` is `found`."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The profile URL, when found."},"experience":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Work-history entries included."},"posts":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Recent posts included."}},"required":["status","matchedBy","linkedinUrl","experience","posts"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Whether the LinkedIn profile from Trayo's own data went into the prompt, and how much of it."}},"required":["trayo","linkedin"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"A receipt of what the answer was grounded in. Read it before trusting an assured-sounding answer, and to decide what to look up or link before asking again."}},"required":["personId","question","intent","answer","gaps","sources"],"additionalProperties":false},"FindRequestDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"target":{"type":"string","enum":["companies","people"],"description":"What to return: `companies` or `people`. People are returned with their current company."},"mode":{"description":"Use `direct` for a bounded search of stored company and people data with exact `filters`. No sentence interpretation or external search. Requires filters, rejects query, and returns one page of at most 100 records. Company records without websites are kept and can be searched again by companyIds. A direct title search checks at most 30 companies per request in `sort` order (largest by default, smallest with `smallest_first`): send at most 30 companyIds or companyWebsites at a time. Omit mode to retain the usual query or filters search.","type":"string","const":"direct"},"query":{"description":"A sentence describing what to find. Trayo interprets it and ranks results by relevance; `interpreted` in the response says what it read. Omit it to search by `filters` alone, which is exact and paginated. Send both to rank by the sentence and keep only rows that pass the filters. On a people search, the company filters (`companyWebsites`, `companyIds`, `industries`, `headcount`, `hq` and the rest) are checked against each ranked person's current employer, which the row then carries: `companyWebsites` must be that employer's own website. A person whose employer fails them, or whose current employer Trayo cannot identify, is left out. The page is not refilled, and a `query_page_filtered` note counts who was left out. For a filters-only people search, omit `query` and use `mode: \"direct\"` with `filters.title` and named companies or an indexed company filter such as `industries` or `headcount`; country, founded-year, technology and company-type filters alone are insufficient beside a title. A title-free companies search can use those filters alone, checking a bounded set of the largest companies (`led_by_headcount`).","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":1000},"filters":{"description":"Exact attributes to match. Never interpreted, never fail-open. Alone, `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` and `headcount` select the set and the rest narrow it; a companies search without `title` may send only a founded year, company type, technology or place filter, then checks the largest companies (`led_by_headcount` in `notes`). With a `query`, any combination narrows the ranked results.","type":"object","properties":{"industries":{"description":"Exact values from the industry taxonomy — list them with `GET /v1/find/industries?contains=software`. \"SaaS\" is not a value; \"software development\" is. Case-insensitive, up to 99. Several values are a union. An uncapped page merges the same rows each value returns alone; when `truncated` is true, read the notes before treating the page as complete. When present, industries drive the indexed scan. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":99,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"headcount":{"description":"Inclusive employee-count band, e.g. `{\"min\": 11, \"max\": 200}`. Either bound may be omitted. Selects a set on its own. In a filters-only search with no company attributes, or beside a title without an indexed company filter, send a saved workspace profile's `buyerProfile.sizeBand` (its max capped at 50,000) beside its mapped industries; with a partial profile, no mapped industry or no profile, send `{\"min\": 1, \"max\": 10000}`. Never `{\"min\": 1}` alone: the largest employers make common titles time out. Beside a title with only a founded year, technology or company type, fit the band to it (founded in recent years: `{\"min\": 1, \"max\": 50}`). Funding stage needs an indexed company filter beside it. Without a title, founded year, technology and company type may go alone. With a country and no industries, pin its main cities or states: the band beside a country alone can time out. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":10000000},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10000000}},"additionalProperties":false},"hq":{"description":"Where the company is headquartered, not where any person is based. For where a person is based, use `filters.person` on a people search (`target: people`, no `query`).","type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"description":"Countries of the headquarters: ISO-2 codes (`[\"US\", \"DE\"]`) or country names (`[\"United States\", \"Germany\"]`), any case. A name is read as its ISO-2 code, and `applied` echoes the codes. Applied inside the scan, cheap, any number up to 60. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":60,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":80}},"regions":{"description":"Shorthand for a block of countries: `north_america`, `europe`, `asia_pacific`, `latin_america`, `middle_east`, `africa`. Expands to ISO-2 codes and merges with `countries`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":6,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["north_america","europe","asia_pacific","latin_america","middle_east","africa"]}},"cities":{"description":"Headquarters city, exact match, case-insensitive (`\"san francisco\"`). At most 20. Can select a set on its own. A bare city name is a NAME, not a place, and matches it everywhere on earth: `\"dublin\"` is Ireland and Ohio and California, `\"newark\"` is New Jersey and California. Pin one by writing `\"city|state\"`, `\"city|state|country\"` or `\"city||country\"` — `\"newark|california\"`, `\"dublin||IE\"`, `\"richmond|virginia|US\"`. The state is its full name and the country an ISO-2 code, each pinned city narrows on its own, and pinned and bare values mix freely. When a bare value turns out to span more than one place, the response says so in `notes`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"states":{"description":"Headquarters state or province, full name, case-insensitive (`\"california\"`, `\"bavaria\"`, not `\"CA\"`). At most 20. Can select a set on its own. There is no US-region shorthand; spell the states out. Applied alongside `cities` as a separate AND, so `cities: [\"newark\", \"dublin\"]` with `states: [\"california\", \"ohio\"]` admits all four pairings — write the pins into `cities` to avoid that. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}}},"additionalProperties":false},"person":{"description":"`target: people` without `query` only. Where the person says they are, from their LinkedIn profile location: not a verified address, and not the employer's headquarters (that is `hq`). `countries`, `states` and `cities` are each any-of, and they AND together. A person with no recorded value for a part you sent does NOT match. `states` and `cities` narrow the people at a set of companies: send them beside `companyWebsites`, `companyIds` or an indexed company filter (`industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states`, `headcount`). Applied before `perCompany`, so each company still returns its most senior people who match.","type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"description":"Countries the person is in: ISO-2 codes (`[\"US\", \"DE\"]`) or country names (`[\"United States\", \"Germany\"]`), any case. A name is read as its ISO-2 code, and `applied` echoes the codes. At most 60.","minItems":1,"maxItems":60,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":80}},"states":{"description":"State or province the person is in, full name, case-insensitive (`\"california\"`, not `\"CA\"`). At most 20.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"cities":{"description":"City the person is in, whole name, case-insensitive (`\"san diego\"`). A person whose profile names only a metro area (\"Greater San Diego Area\") has no city and does not match one. A city name can exist in several places: send `countries` or `states` beside it. At most 20.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}}},"additionalProperties":false},"fundingStages":{"description":"The most recent funding round Trayo recorded, as a closed set: `pre_seed`, `seed`, `series_a`, `series_b`, `series_c`, `series_d_plus`, `private_equity`, `debt`, `post_ipo`, `grant`. Only a minority of companies carry funding data at all, so this keeps ONLY companies with a recorded round of that kind — an unfunded or unrecorded company never matches. It is a record, not a stage of life: an old or listed company can carry a stale `seed`. For \"early-stage\", pair it with `foundedYear` and `headcount`. With a `query`, the sentence path's notion of stage is checked against this record and loses: a company recorded as \"Series B\" that has since raised a Series C is dropped. Narrows a set selected by `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount`; a funding stage alone cannot select companies or people.","minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["pre_seed","seed","series_a","series_b","series_c","series_d_plus","private_equity","debt","post_ipo","grant"]}},"foundedYear":{"description":"Inclusive founded-year band, e.g. `{\"min\": 2018}`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":1000,"maximum":2100},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":1000,"maximum":2100}},"additionalProperties":false},"technologies":{"description":"Technologies detected on the company's public web presence, any of, up to 20. Matched on the name with punctuation and case removed, so `\"Amazon Web Services\"`, `\"aws\"` and `\"HubSpot\"` are all valid. A web-stack scan: good for marketing, analytics and cloud tooling, blind to internal systems such as an ERP. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":80}},"companyTypes":{"description":"Legal form as the company describes itself, a closed set: `privately held`, `public company`, `nonprofit`, `educational`, `government agency`, `partnership`, `self-employed`, `self-owned`. `public company` includes wholly-owned subsidiaries of listed groups, not only the listed entity. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":8,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["privately held","public company","nonprofit","educational","government agency","partnership","self-employed","self-owned"]}},"companyIds":{"description":"**Reach for `companyWebsites` instead** — it names the same companies by something you already have. This is the older form of that filter, kept for callers holding ids: exact companies, by the `id` a companies search returned with `known: true`, at most 200. For `target: companies` it is a lookup of exactly those rows; for `target: people` it names whose people to search. Other company filters narrow only these companies (AND). Without `query`, `named_companies_filtered` notes identify companies the attributes exclude. With `query`, the ranked candidates are intersected with these IDs; this does not search every named company. Do not combine with `companyWebsites`.","minItems":1,"maxItems":200,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":18,"pattern":"^[1-9]\\d*$"}},"companyWebsites":{"description":"The same thing as `companyIds`, named by something you already have: each company's own website — at most 100. `stripe.com`, `www.stripe.com` and `https://stripe.com/pricing` all name the same company; the scheme, `www.` and any path are ignored. Without `query`, each website is resolved to exactly one company Trayo holds, and the resolution is reported back in `resolvedWebsites` so you can see which company each one became. Websites that do not resolve are left out of the search and listed in `unresolvedWebsites`, so the answer can cover fewer companies than you named: read that list before you treat a short page as complete. A `not_found` one: check the spelling, send the company's own domain rather than a parent group's, a reseller's or a marketing microsite's, or describe the company with `filters.industries`, `filters.hq` or `filters.headcount` in a separate search. An `uncertain` one: the domain carries several possible companies and none was searched; show its `alternatives` to the user, or search again with `filters.companyIds` set to the right alternative's `id`. Only when NONE of the websites resolves is the answer a `404 company_not_found` naming them. Other company filters narrow only the resolved companies (AND), and `named_companies_filtered` notes identify companies they exclude. On a people search with `query`, websites instead match the ranked people's current employers directly: `resolvedWebsites` and `unresolvedWebsites` are null, and no matching employer yields an empty page rather than `company_not_found`. A `query_page_filtered` note counts people left out; the page is not refilled. Other company attributes also narrow the current employer (AND). Do not combine with `companyIds`.","minItems":1,"maxItems":100,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":4,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[Hh][Tt][Tt][Pp][Ss]?:\\/\\/)?[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?)+\\.?(?:[/?#].*)?$"}},"title":{"description":"Current employee title. A filters-only people search requires `title.any` or `title.all`, even with `companyIds` or `companyWebsites`; no-title named-company sampling is unsupported. In `mode: direct`, a companies search may also use it: return each company with at least one matching current employee. Pair it with companyIds, companyWebsites or an indexed company filter; when the request names none, take them from the workspace profile (see `headcount`). A title is searched only at the first companies in `sort` order (in `mode: direct`, at most 30: largest first by default, the smallest with `smallest_first`) unless `notes` say `title_matched_first`; `company_fan_out_limit_reached` says more companies matched. Never report it as every company.","type":"object","properties":{"any":{"description":"Title terms, ANY of which must appear in the person's current title. A multi-word term matches as a phrase, with at most one word between the words: `\"vp finance\"` matches \"VP Finance\" and \"VP of Finance\", not \"VP of Engineering, Finance partner\". Punctuation and case are ignored: `\"founder\"` matches \"Co-Founder & CEO\". A single common word matches across every function (`\"automation\"` finds manufacturing and procurement automation too) — prefer two-word terms and use `exclude`. An acronym is matched LITERALLY and expands to nothing: `\"cto\"` does not match \"Chief Technology Officer\", and the same role is written both ways inside one company, so send both forms — `[\"cto\", \"chief technology officer\"]`. At named companies (`companyIds`, `companyWebsites`) every term is searched again at each company, so a long list there can run out of time: send the forms the user means, not every variant.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"all":{"description":"Title terms that must ALL appear. Combine with `any` freely.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"exclude":{"description":"Title terms that disqualify a person (`[\"assistant\", \"intern\"]`).","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}}},"additionalProperties":false},"excludeWorkspace":{"description":"`target: companies` only. Default false. When true, companies already in your workspace are left out of `data` instead of coming back as `state: alreadyAdded`, so the page fills with companies you do not have. Use this for \"find N new companies\". \"Already in your workspace\" is decided exactly as `state` is. It is not a filter on its own: a filters-only search still needs a company filter. On a filters-only search a page can come back shorter than `limit` with `hasMore: true` when most matches were already yours — keep paging with `nextCursor`.","type":"boolean"}},"additionalProperties":false},"sort":{"description":"Filters-only. Page order by company headcount: `largest_first` (the default) or `smallest_first`. `smallest_first` needs `filters.headcount.min` of at least 1, because companies with no recorded headcount would otherwise come first. A search whose title is rare is ordered by company id instead and ignores `sort`; its pages carry a `title_matched_first` note. A search with a `query` is ranked by relevance and takes no `sort`.","type":"string","enum":["largest_first","smallest_first"]},"perCompany":{"description":"`target: people`, filters-only. People read per company before paging, most senior title first. Default 4, at most 25. Keeps one large employer from filling every page.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":25},"limit":{"description":"Page size. Default 25, at most 100. A search with a `query` returns at most 50.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":100},"cursor":{"description":"The `nextCursor` from the previous page, sent back verbatim WITH THE SAME REQUEST. Filters-only searches page; a search with a `query` returns one ranked page and rejects a cursor.","type":"string","maxLength":64}},"required":["target"],"examples":[{"target":"people","filters":{"industries":["software development"],"hq":{"regions":["europe"]},"headcount":{"min":10,"max":200},"fundingStages":["seed","series_a"],"foundedYear":{"min":2019},"title":{"any":["founder","cto"],"exclude":["assistant"]}},"perCompany":2,"limit":25}],"additionalProperties":false},"FindResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"target":{"type":"string","enum":["companies","people"]},"data":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Identifies this row: the Trayo company id when `known` is true, otherwise an id unique to this response only. You do not have to carry it around — to look these companies up again, or to search their people, send `website` back as `filters.companyWebsites`. For a direct record without a website, send its id as filters.companyIds."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"website":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Primary website host, e.g. `stripe.com`. Send it back as `filters.companyWebsites` to search this company again, with no `query` beside it. Null when Trayo holds no website for it."},"url":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"`https://` + `website`, ready to send as `url` to `POST /v1/accounts`. Null when the website is unknown."},"logoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company logo URL, derived from `website`, so null when the website is unknown. Usable directly as an `<img src>` and needs no API key; it answers 404 when no logo resolves."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"industry":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The taxonomy value, as Trayo holds it."},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}]},"hq":{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO-2."}},"required":["city","state","country"],"additionalProperties":false},"fundingStage":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"A `fundingStages` id when the most recent recorded round maps to one, else the value Trayo holds, verbatim. Null when no round is recorded."},"foundedYear":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}]},"companyType":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"One of `companyTypes`, or null."},"description":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The description Trayo holds, cut at 500 characters."},"why":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why this company matched the `query`, in Trayo's words. Null on a filters-only search."},"known":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether this row is backed by a profile Trayo holds. Always true on a filters-only search. On a sentence search, false means the search found a company Trayo does not hold: the attributes are as the search reported them, `id` is not one of our own ids, and — because filters are exact — the row would not have survived any `filters`."},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["selectable","alreadyAdded"],"description":"`alreadyAdded` means this record is already in your workspace."}},"required":["id","name","website","url","logoUrl","linkedinUrl","industry","headcount","hq","fundingStage","foundedYear","companyType","description","why","known","state"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Trayo person id on a filters-only search; an id unique to this response on a sentence search."},"fullName":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Current title, as it is recorded for them."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile. Send it to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup`, `POST /v1/research/person`, or as `linkedinUrl` to `POST /v1/people`."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"City, e.g. `San Diego`. Null when the profile names only a region or metro area."},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"State, province or region, e.g. `California`."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, e.g. `US`."},"raw":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The location as written on the profile, e.g. `Greater San Diego Area`. Usually present; the only populated field when the profile names just a metro area."}},"required":["city","state","country","raw"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where the person says they are on their LinkedIn profile. Usually their home metro area, but it is the person's own statement, not a verified address, and it is not where their company is headquartered. Null when Trayo has no location for them. Filled on a filters-only search; null on a sentence search."},"company":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Trayo company id, when known."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"website":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Primary website host."},"logoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company logo URL, derived from `website`. Null when the website is unknown."},"industry":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}]},"hq":{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"ISO-2."}},"required":["city","state","country"],"additionalProperties":false},"fundingStage":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"A `fundingStages` id when the most recent recorded round maps to one, else the value Trayo holds, verbatim. Null when no round is recorded."},"foundedYear":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["id","name","website","logoUrl","industry","headcount","hq","fundingStage","foundedYear"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their current employer, with the attributes the company filters read, so a row can be checked against the request. Complete on a filters-only search, and on a sentence search sent with company filters, where it is the employer those filters were checked against. On a sentence search without them usually name-only, because a sentence search returns a company reference without a website."},"state":{"type":"string","enum":["selectable","alreadyAdded"],"description":"`alreadyAdded` means this record is already in your workspace."}},"required":["id","fullName","title","linkedinUrl","linkedinUsername","photoUrl","location","company","state"],"additionalProperties":false}}],"description":"Companies or people, per `target`. Filters-only pages are ordered by company headcount (`sort`), and people within a company most senior first — except a search whose title is read first, which is ordered by company id and says so in `notes` (`title_matched_first`, or `title_matches_sampled` for a page checked against a sample of the title's roles). An empty array is a successful search that matched nothing, unless `truncated` is true."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"A next page exists. People pages fill company by company, so a page can end before every matched company has been reached — keep paging until false. Always false on a sentence search, which returns one ranked page."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"truncated":{"type":"boolean","description":"The result is or may be incomplete. On a sentence search: more candidates than the page. On a filters-only search: the scan reached its cap, possibly even on a full page, so the pages you can reach are only the companies reached within the cap, not all of the set — `notes` says when that cap was the per-value budget of a multi-value filter, and which values ran out. Either way the answer is to narrow, not to page deeper. In direct people searches, also true when an employer reached perCompany; additional matches may exist there, and raising perCompany can reveal them. Also true when the page was checked against a sample of the title's roles (`title_matches_sampled`)."},"degraded":{"type":"boolean","description":"Part of this sentence search did not answer, so a thin result may not mean a genuine absence of matches. Always false on a filters-only search."},"droppedUnaddressable":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Company rows this search found and then dropped for carrying neither a website nor a `url`. `POST /v1/accounts` would accept such a row — `url` is optional there — but deduplication keys on the website, and a sentence match with no website at all is overwhelmingly a directory, a job board or an institution rather than a company. They are dropped on that quality ground, so every row left in `data` can be acted on. On a search with a `query` they are dropped BEFORE the page is filled, so the page still reaches its size; on a filters-only search the page has already been read at its size, so a dropped row shortens it and `hasMore` is unaffected. A high number against a thin `data` means the sentence matched directories, job boards and institutions rather than companies — name the kind of company you want. Always 0 for `target: people`, whose rows are addressed by `linkedinUrl`, and in mode: direct, which keeps company records without websites and identifies them by id."},"notes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"code":{"type":"string","enum":["filter_values_truncated","city_spans_multiple_places","search_degraded","per_company_limit_reached","title_matched_first","title_matches_sampled","search_time_limit_reached","residual_scan_limit_reached","company_fan_out_limit_reached","named_companies_filtered","led_by_headcount","query_page_filtered"],"description":"What the search could not do exactly. `filter_values_truncated`: the scan budget is shared between the values of one filter and ran out, so the result may omit matches — split the request, one value per call, and merge the pages. `city_spans_multiple_places`: a bare city name in `hq.cities` matched more than one state or country on this page — pin it (`\"newark|california\"`) if you meant one. `per_company_limit_reached`: a direct people search returned the perCompany cap for at least one employer; additional matching people may exist there. `title_matched_first`: the title was rare, so every current role it matches was read and checked against the company filters — paging to the end returns every match, at most `perCompany` per company — and pages are ordered by company id, not headcount (`sort` does not apply). `title_matches_sampled`: the title matched more current roles than one search reads, so the page was checked against the roles it did read, a sample rather than every match; `truncated` is true and there is no next page. Fewer or more specific title terms read every match. `search_time_limit_reached`: the time limit stopped a people search after some companies; the returned matches are partial, there is no next page, and values lists the remaining company IDs. `residual_scan_limit_reached`: the named filter (funding stage, founded year, technologies or company type; also country beside `led_by_headcount`) is checked company by company, and the search stopped after checking the first companies selected by other filters in `sort` order; add industries, a headcount band or cities to check the companies you want. `company_fan_out_limit_reached`: titles were searched only at the first companies that passed the company filters in `sort` order; narrow those filters, or send fewer companies per request. `named_companies_filtered`: company attributes excluded one or more explicitly named companies; the note identifies them, while `applied` shows the filters that were enforced. `led_by_headcount`: no filter could select the companies (a companies search with only a founded year, company type, technologies or country), so they were taken largest first and checked one by one, up to a fixed number: every row matches, but these are the largest matches, not all of them; tell the user. `query_page_filtered`: a people search with a `query` checked each ranked person's current employer against the company filters and left out the people whose employer failed them or could not be identified; the page was not refilled, so it can be short. `mode: \"direct\"` with `filters.title` and no `query` searches using exact filters instead; it also needs named companies or an indexed company filter such as `industries` or `headcount`. `search_degraded`: this response carries `degraded: true`. One data source this search depends on was unavailable, slow or switched off for this workspace, so this answer may be thinner than usual — read a short result as possibly incomplete rather than as an absence of matches. A repeat of this request is billed as a new search, and some causes persist for a workspace and degrade every repeat, so use what came back or narrow the request rather than resending it."},"filter":{"description":"The request field the note is about, e.g. `filters.industries`. Absent when the note is about the response as a whole rather than one field, which is the case for `search_degraded` — group notes by this field only when it is present.","type":"string"},"values":{"description":"The values of that field the note applies to, as they were sent. Absent whenever `filter` is.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"detail":{"type":"string","description":"One sentence naming what was found, for a human reading the response."}},"required":["code","detail"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Everything about this answer that is not simply \"here are the matches\": a filter whose values were not all scanned to the end, a bare city name that turned out to mean several places, a sentence search that came back `degraded` because a data source it depends on did not answer. Empty on a search with nothing to report, which is the usual case. Read it before treating a short result as an absence of matches: it is where a short page says it was cut off, or thinned by an outage, rather than that nothing matched."},"applied":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"industries":{"description":"Exact values from the industry taxonomy — list them with `GET /v1/find/industries?contains=software`. \"SaaS\" is not a value; \"software development\" is. Case-insensitive, up to 99. Several values are a union. An uncapped page merges the same rows each value returns alone; when `truncated` is true, read the notes before treating the page as complete. When present, industries drive the indexed scan. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":99,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"headcount":{"description":"Inclusive employee-count band, e.g. `{\"min\": 11, \"max\": 200}`. Either bound may be omitted. Selects a set on its own. In a filters-only search with no company attributes, or beside a title without an indexed company filter, send a saved workspace profile's `buyerProfile.sizeBand` (its max capped at 50,000) beside its mapped industries; with a partial profile, no mapped industry or no profile, send `{\"min\": 1, \"max\": 10000}`. Never `{\"min\": 1}` alone: the largest employers make common titles time out. Beside a title with only a founded year, technology or company type, fit the band to it (founded in recent years: `{\"min\": 1, \"max\": 50}`). Funding stage needs an indexed company filter beside it. Without a title, founded year, technology and company type may go alone. With a country and no industries, pin its main cities or states: the band beside a country alone can time out. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":10000000},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10000000}},"additionalProperties":false},"hq":{"description":"Where the company is headquartered, not where any person is based. For where a person is based, use `filters.person` on a people search (`target: people`, no `query`).","type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"description":"Countries of the headquarters: ISO-2 codes (`[\"US\", \"DE\"]`) or country names (`[\"United States\", \"Germany\"]`), any case. A name is read as its ISO-2 code, and `applied` echoes the codes. Applied inside the scan, cheap, any number up to 60. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":60,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":80}},"regions":{"description":"Shorthand for a block of countries: `north_america`, `europe`, `asia_pacific`, `latin_america`, `middle_east`, `africa`. Expands to ISO-2 codes and merges with `countries`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":6,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["north_america","europe","asia_pacific","latin_america","middle_east","africa"]}},"cities":{"description":"Headquarters city, exact match, case-insensitive (`\"san francisco\"`). At most 20. Can select a set on its own. A bare city name is a NAME, not a place, and matches it everywhere on earth: `\"dublin\"` is Ireland and Ohio and California, `\"newark\"` is New Jersey and California. Pin one by writing `\"city|state\"`, `\"city|state|country\"` or `\"city||country\"` — `\"newark|california\"`, `\"dublin||IE\"`, `\"richmond|virginia|US\"`. The state is its full name and the country an ISO-2 code, each pinned city narrows on its own, and pinned and bare values mix freely. When a bare value turns out to span more than one place, the response says so in `notes`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"states":{"description":"Headquarters state or province, full name, case-insensitive (`\"california\"`, `\"bavaria\"`, not `\"CA\"`). At most 20. Can select a set on its own. There is no US-region shorthand; spell the states out. Applied alongside `cities` as a separate AND, so `cities: [\"newark\", \"dublin\"]` with `states: [\"california\", \"ohio\"]` admits all four pairings — write the pins into `cities` to avoid that. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}}},"additionalProperties":false},"person":{"description":"`target: people` without `query` only. Where the person says they are, from their LinkedIn profile location: not a verified address, and not the employer's headquarters (that is `hq`). `countries`, `states` and `cities` are each any-of, and they AND together. A person with no recorded value for a part you sent does NOT match. `states` and `cities` narrow the people at a set of companies: send them beside `companyWebsites`, `companyIds` or an indexed company filter (`industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states`, `headcount`). Applied before `perCompany`, so each company still returns its most senior people who match.","type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"description":"Countries the person is in: ISO-2 codes (`[\"US\", \"DE\"]`) or country names (`[\"United States\", \"Germany\"]`), any case. A name is read as its ISO-2 code, and `applied` echoes the codes. At most 60.","minItems":1,"maxItems":60,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":80}},"states":{"description":"State or province the person is in, full name, case-insensitive (`\"california\"`, not `\"CA\"`). At most 20.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}},"cities":{"description":"City the person is in, whole name, case-insensitive (`\"san diego\"`). A person whose profile names only a metro area (\"Greater San Diego Area\") has no city and does not match one. A city name can exist in several places: send `countries` or `states` beside it. At most 20.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100}}},"additionalProperties":false},"fundingStages":{"description":"The most recent funding round Trayo recorded, as a closed set: `pre_seed`, `seed`, `series_a`, `series_b`, `series_c`, `series_d_plus`, `private_equity`, `debt`, `post_ipo`, `grant`. Only a minority of companies carry funding data at all, so this keeps ONLY companies with a recorded round of that kind — an unfunded or unrecorded company never matches. It is a record, not a stage of life: an old or listed company can carry a stale `seed`. For \"early-stage\", pair it with `foundedYear` and `headcount`. With a `query`, the sentence path's notion of stage is checked against this record and loses: a company recorded as \"Series B\" that has since raised a Series C is dropped. Narrows a set selected by `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount`; a funding stage alone cannot select companies or people.","minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["pre_seed","seed","series_a","series_b","series_c","series_d_plus","private_equity","debt","post_ipo","grant"]}},"foundedYear":{"description":"Inclusive founded-year band, e.g. `{\"min\": 2018}`. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":1000,"maximum":2100},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":1000,"maximum":2100}},"additionalProperties":false},"technologies":{"description":"Technologies detected on the company's public web presence, any of, up to 20. Matched on the name with punctuation and case removed, so `\"Amazon Web Services\"`, `\"aws\"` and `\"HubSpot\"` are all valid. A web-stack scan: good for marketing, analytics and cloud tooling, blind to internal systems such as an ERP. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":80}},"companyTypes":{"description":"Legal form as the company describes itself, a closed set: `privately held`, `public company`, `nonprofit`, `educational`, `government agency`, `partnership`, `self-employed`, `self-owned`. `public company` includes wholly-owned subsidiaries of listed groups, not only the listed entity. Exact: a company with no value for this attribute does NOT match. Narrows a set. Sent with no `industries`, `hq.cities`, `hq.states` or `headcount` and no `query`, a companies search checks companies with known headcount largest first, up to a fixed number, and `notes` say `led_by_headcount`: the largest matches, not all of them. A search with `filters.title` needs one of those four beside it.","minItems":1,"maxItems":8,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["privately held","public company","nonprofit","educational","government agency","partnership","self-employed","self-owned"]}},"companyIds":{"description":"**Reach for `companyWebsites` instead** — it names the same companies by something you already have. This is the older form of that filter, kept for callers holding ids: exact companies, by the `id` a companies search returned with `known: true`, at most 200. For `target: companies` it is a lookup of exactly those rows; for `target: people` it names whose people to search. Other company filters narrow only these companies (AND). Without `query`, `named_companies_filtered` notes identify companies the attributes exclude. With `query`, the ranked candidates are intersected with these IDs; this does not search every named company. Do not combine with `companyWebsites`.","minItems":1,"maxItems":200,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":18,"pattern":"^[1-9]\\d*$"}},"companyWebsites":{"description":"The same thing as `companyIds`, named by something you already have: each company's own website — at most 100. `stripe.com`, `www.stripe.com` and `https://stripe.com/pricing` all name the same company; the scheme, `www.` and any path are ignored. Without `query`, each website is resolved to exactly one company Trayo holds, and the resolution is reported back in `resolvedWebsites` so you can see which company each one became. Websites that do not resolve are left out of the search and listed in `unresolvedWebsites`, so the answer can cover fewer companies than you named: read that list before you treat a short page as complete. A `not_found` one: check the spelling, send the company's own domain rather than a parent group's, a reseller's or a marketing microsite's, or describe the company with `filters.industries`, `filters.hq` or `filters.headcount` in a separate search. An `uncertain` one: the domain carries several possible companies and none was searched; show its `alternatives` to the user, or search again with `filters.companyIds` set to the right alternative's `id`. Only when NONE of the websites resolves is the answer a `404 company_not_found` naming them. Other company filters narrow only the resolved companies (AND), and `named_companies_filtered` notes identify companies they exclude. On a people search with `query`, websites instead match the ranked people's current employers directly: `resolvedWebsites` and `unresolvedWebsites` are null, and no matching employer yields an empty page rather than `company_not_found`. A `query_page_filtered` note counts people left out; the page is not refilled. Other company attributes also narrow the current employer (AND). Do not combine with `companyIds`.","minItems":1,"maxItems":100,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":4,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[Hh][Tt][Tt][Pp][Ss]?:\\/\\/)?[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?)+\\.?(?:[/?#].*)?$"}},"title":{"description":"Current employee title. A filters-only people search requires `title.any` or `title.all`, even with `companyIds` or `companyWebsites`; no-title named-company sampling is unsupported. In `mode: direct`, a companies search may also use it: return each company with at least one matching current employee. Pair it with companyIds, companyWebsites or an indexed company filter; when the request names none, take them from the workspace profile (see `headcount`). A title is searched only at the first companies in `sort` order (in `mode: direct`, at most 30: largest first by default, the smallest with `smallest_first`) unless `notes` say `title_matched_first`; `company_fan_out_limit_reached` says more companies matched. Never report it as every company.","type":"object","properties":{"any":{"description":"Title terms, ANY of which must appear in the person's current title. A multi-word term matches as a phrase, with at most one word between the words: `\"vp finance\"` matches \"VP Finance\" and \"VP of Finance\", not \"VP of Engineering, Finance partner\". Punctuation and case are ignored: `\"founder\"` matches \"Co-Founder & CEO\". A single common word matches across every function (`\"automation\"` finds manufacturing and procurement automation too) — prefer two-word terms and use `exclude`. An acronym is matched LITERALLY and expands to nothing: `\"cto\"` does not match \"Chief Technology Officer\", and the same role is written both ways inside one company, so send both forms — `[\"cto\", \"chief technology officer\"]`. At named companies (`companyIds`, `companyWebsites`) every term is searched again at each company, so a long list there can run out of time: send the forms the user means, not every variant.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"all":{"description":"Title terms that must ALL appear. Combine with `any` freely.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"exclude":{"description":"Title terms that disqualify a person (`[\"assistant\", \"intern\"]`).","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}}},"additionalProperties":false},"excludeWorkspace":{"description":"`target: companies` only. Default false. When true, companies already in your workspace are left out of `data` instead of coming back as `state: alreadyAdded`, so the page fills with companies you do not have. Use this for \"find N new companies\". \"Already in your workspace\" is decided exactly as `state` is. It is not a filter on its own: a filters-only search still needs a company filter. On a filters-only search a page can come back shorter than `limit` with `hasMore: true` when most matches were already yours — keep paging with `nextCursor`.","type":"boolean"}},"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The `filters` that were enforced on this response, as sent, except that `hq.countries` holds the ISO-2 code each value was read as (`\"United States\"` comes back as `\"US\"`). Every row in `data` passed all of them. Null when no filters were sent."},"resolvedWebsites":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"website":{"type":"string","description":"The website as it was matched: lowercased, without the scheme, `www.` or any path."},"id":{"type":"string","description":"The company profile this website resolved to, so two entries can be told apart. Read `name` to check the pick; you name companies to this route by their websites."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That company, as Trayo names it."},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Its headcount. When the domain carries several profiles whose names match it, the largest wins."}},"required":["website","id","name","headcount"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"What each `filters.companyWebsites` value became, one entry per distinct website, in the order you sent them. Null when no websites were sent, and on a people search with a `query`, which matches each website against the employer of every ranked person instead. A domain can carry more than one company profile — a squatter, a regional page, a subsidiary — so a website resolves only when a profile's name matches the domain and no much larger profile under another name sits beside it; the largest such profile wins. Anything less clear is listed in `unresolvedWebsites` as `uncertain`, with the candidates. Read this before you trust the page: if a `name` here is not the company you meant, the rows under it are not either. To search a different profile, send its id in `filters.companyIds` instead. Lists only the websites that resolved: these are exactly the companies searched."},"unresolvedWebsites":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"website":{"type":"string","description":"The website as it was matched: lowercased, without the scheme, `www.` or any path. A value with no usable host is echoed as you sent it."},"reason":{"type":"string","enum":["not_found","uncertain"],"description":"Why it was left out. `not_found`: Trayo holds no company on this domain, or the value has no usable host. Check the spelling, send the company's own domain, or describe the company with filters instead. `uncertain`: Trayo holds company profiles on this domain, but none clearly is the company the website belongs to (no name matching the domain, or a much larger profile under another name beside it), so none was searched rather than risk the wrong one. The candidates are in `alternatives`: show them to the user, or, if one is clearly right, search again with `filters.companyIds` set to that alternative's `id`. New reasons may be added; treat an unknown one as not searched."},"alternatives":{"description":"Only for `reason: \"uncertain\"`: the profiles this website could name, at most 3, largest first. Absent for other reasons.","maxItems":3,"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"The company id. Send it in `filters.companyIds` (instead of `filters.companyWebsites`) to search this exact company."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"That company, as Trayo names it."},"headcount":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Its headcount, when known."}},"required":["id","name","headcount"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["website","reason"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The `filters.companyWebsites` values that did not resolve to a company and were therefore NOT searched, one entry per distinct website, in the order you sent them: `not_found` (no company on that domain) or `uncertain` (several possible companies, listed in `alternatives`). Empty when every website resolved; null when no websites were sent, and on a people search with a `query`. When this is not empty, `data` covers only the companies in `resolvedWebsites`: a short page here is not a sign that the missing companies have nobody matching. For an `uncertain` website, show the alternatives to the user, or search again with `filters.companyIds` set to the right alternative's `id`."},"interpreted":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","description":"How the sentence was rewritten before searching."},"filters":{"type":"object","properties":{"industries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"headcount":{"type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991}},"additionalProperties":false},"hq":{"type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"cities":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"states":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"additionalProperties":false},"fundingStages":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"technologies":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"What Trayo read out of the sentence as a constraint, in the request's own `filters` shape. Send it back as `filters` (with or without the `query`) to make the search exact and reproducible. A criterion in your sentence that is not here was NOT enforced; it only steered the rewritten query."}},"required":["query","filters"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Present when a `query` was sent; null on a filters-only search."}},"required":["target","data","hasMore","nextCursor","truncated","degraded","droppedUnaddressable","notes","applied","resolvedWebsites","unresolvedWebsites","interpreted"],"additionalProperties":false},"IndustriesResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"data":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Industry values, lowercase, exactly as `filters.industries` expects them."}},"required":["data"],"additionalProperties":false},"JobChangeSearchBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"source":{"description":"The company the person left.","type":"object","properties":{"companyId":{"description":"Trayo's own id for the company, as a number or numeric string: the `companyId` a post, post-engager or person-research result carries, or the `id` of a company find result. Not a workspace account UUID. When sent, it is the key: the company is read by this id and nothing else is tried, so an id Trayo does not hold is `company_not_found`. A `name`, `website` or `linkedinUrl` sent beside it is not used.","anyOf":[{"type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"string","pattern":"^[1-9]\\d*$"}]},"accountId":{"description":"An account in your workspace, on its own. Its existing match is used when it has one; otherwise the account is resolved from its website, LinkedIn handle and name exactly as the app would, so an account we have not yet matched still works when the company is in our data.","type":"string","pattern":"^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$","format":"uuid"},"website":{"description":"The company's own website, as a URL or a domain (`https://zapier.com`, `zapier.com`). The strongest key after the LinkedIn page: it is matched against our data, then, on a miss, followed through a cross-domain redirect, so a legacy or vanity domain that forwards to the current site still resolves. Sent on its own as the bare site (no path or subdomain), a data or social platform's domain names the platform itself (`zoominfo.com` is ZoomInfo); a page on such a site (`linkedin.com/company/acme`) is not a company website. When our data files the company under a different website, a website sent on its own is also tried as the LinkedIn page it spells (`gong.io` as `gong-io`), accepted only for an established company whose name matches the domain. An alias that does not forward to the company's current site is a miss: send the current website, the LinkedIn page or the name. Does not have to be in your workspace.","type":"string","minLength":3,"maxLength":2048},"linkedinUrl":{"description":"The company's LinkedIn page — the `linkedinUrl` a find result or a person row carries, copied through (`https://www.linkedin.com/company/zapier`); the bare handle (`zapier`) is accepted too. Tried first when present, and the most precise key there is. Showcase pages are not company pages: send the company's own. Copy the handle from a Trayo result or the company's own site rather than guessing it from the name — a slug that belongs to a different company resolves to that company.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2048},"name":{"description":"The company name. Tried last and the weakest key, since a common name can match the wrong company — send the website when you have it. A name-only lookup finds only companies Trayo holds under that name; an employer that a Trayo person row names with `companyId: null` has no company record, and its name will usually miss.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":200}},"additionalProperties":false},"destination":{"description":"The company the person joined.","type":"object","properties":{"companyId":{"description":"Trayo's own id for the company, as a number or numeric string: the `companyId` a post, post-engager or person-research result carries, or the `id` of a company find result. Not a workspace account UUID. When sent, it is the key: the company is read by this id and nothing else is tried, so an id Trayo does not hold is `company_not_found`. A `name`, `website` or `linkedinUrl` sent beside it is not used.","anyOf":[{"type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"string","pattern":"^[1-9]\\d*$"}]},"accountId":{"description":"An account in your workspace, on its own. Its existing match is used when it has one; otherwise the account is resolved from its website, LinkedIn handle and name exactly as the app would, so an account we have not yet matched still works when the company is in our data.","type":"string","pattern":"^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$","format":"uuid"},"website":{"description":"The company's own website, as a URL or a domain (`https://zapier.com`, `zapier.com`). The strongest key after the LinkedIn page: it is matched against our data, then, on a miss, followed through a cross-domain redirect, so a legacy or vanity domain that forwards to the current site still resolves. Sent on its own as the bare site (no path or subdomain), a data or social platform's domain names the platform itself (`zoominfo.com` is ZoomInfo); a page on such a site (`linkedin.com/company/acme`) is not a company website. When our data files the company under a different website, a website sent on its own is also tried as the LinkedIn page it spells (`gong.io` as `gong-io`), accepted only for an established company whose name matches the domain. An alias that does not forward to the company's current site is a miss: send the current website, the LinkedIn page or the name. Does not have to be in your workspace.","type":"string","minLength":3,"maxLength":2048},"linkedinUrl":{"description":"The company's LinkedIn page — the `linkedinUrl` a find result or a person row carries, copied through (`https://www.linkedin.com/company/zapier`); the bare handle (`zapier`) is accepted too. Tried first when present, and the most precise key there is. Showcase pages are not company pages: send the company's own. Copy the handle from a Trayo result or the company's own site rather than guessing it from the name — a slug that belongs to a different company resolves to that company.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2048},"name":{"description":"The company name. Tried last and the weakest key, since a common name can match the wrong company — send the website when you have it. A name-only lookup finds only companies Trayo holds under that name; an employer that a Trayo person row names with `companyId: null` has no company record, and its name will usually miss.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":200}},"additionalProperties":false},"detectedSince":{"description":"Only moves with `detectedAt >=` this value. It filters `detectedAt` — when Trayo first observed the new role — and NOT `startedAt`, the date the role began; the two can be months apart. To filter on `startedAt`, send `startedSince`; both can be sent, and both apply. The 90-day window starts at midnight UTC 90 days ago; `detectedSince` can only narrow it. An earlier value is clamped to that start, and a future value matches nothing. Destination searches already return the newest moves first: narrowing the window does not recover omitted matches; it returns the same rows or a subset. This is a recency filter, not pagination. Report `hasMore` truncation for the requested period. Send a plain date — `2026-06-07`, read as midnight UTC — or a full ISO-8601 timestamp — `2026-06-07T14:30:00Z` — when you need the time of day. **Relative words are not accepted:** `yesterday`, `last-quarter`, `7d` and anything else that has to be resolved against \"now\" is rejected with a 400 (`invalid_date`). Compute the date yourself and send it.","type":"string"},"seniority":{"description":"Keep only moves INTO a role at this level or above. It reads the title of the role they joined — not the one they left, and not a title list you supply. The four seniority levels widen in order: `cxo` is the C-suite (CEO, CFO, CTO, COO, CMO, CIO, CISO, CRO, CPO and other Chief … Officer titles); `vp` adds every vice-president form (VP, SVP, EVP, Vice President) and President; `director` adds Director; `head` adds Partner, Principal and Head of. With `destination`, sending it WIDENS the search rather than trimming the page: the search reads further through recent moves looking for matches, instead of filtering the same slice it would have read without it. That wider read is still bounded, so a short or empty answer does not prove nobody senior joined, and `hasMore: false` still does not prove completeness. Source-only searches that report `sampled: true` filter the same fixed sample; they do not read further. Matching is heuristic and excludes assistant/support roles. Titles are matched on their words, so a company that names its leaders unusually can come back empty at a level where people do hold the job.","type":"string","enum":["cxo","vp","director","head"]},"limit":{"description":"How many moves to return in this page. Defaults to 25. Values above 100 are capped at 100, not rejected — the same ceiling the other paginating routes in this API use.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"cursor":{"description":"The `nextCursor` from the previous page, sent back verbatim with the SAME search. Omit it for the first page. It resumes the walk where the last page stopped, so a later page neither repeats nor skips what an earlier one returned. It is opaque: do not build or read one. Destination and title searches accept cursors; an indexed source-only search does too. A source-only search may read a fixed sample rather than an ordered walk: when sampled is true, it cannot resume and sending a cursor is rejected. A `title` search pages the same way a destination search does. Changing the search between pages invalidates the cursor.","type":"string"},"title":{"description":"Search WITHOUT a company: moves into a role whose title matches, at any company. Same syntax and matching as `/v1/find` `filters.title`: `any`, `all` and `exclude` terms, matched on the words of the title, and an acronym matches only itself — send `[\"CISO\", \"Chief Information Security Officer\"]`, not one form. Needs `any` or `all`. Cannot be combined with `source` or `destination`; for one company, send it as `destination` and use `seniority`. Reads the moves detected in the same 90-day window, newest first. The title index is read up to 5,000 current roles; a title held by more people than that (`\"cto\"`, `\"director\"`) is searched over part of them and the response says `sampled: true` — narrow it with more specific terms, `all` or `exclude`. More than three `any` terms read fewer roles in total (ten terms: 1,500 between them), taken in table order whichever term matched, so a common term can fill most of the read and leave a rare one few rows: several searches of a few terms each cover every title better than one long list. `seniority`, `startedSince` and `destinationFilters` are applied after that read, so they narrow what survives it, not what is read: they do not make a sampled title complete. Sending `seniority: \"head\"` with a broad term does not keep the term safe — narrow the term itself. For Head of Growth, send `any: [\"Head of Growth\"]`, without the broad alternative `\"Growth\"`. A narrower search may still be sampled; never claim complete coverage.","type":"object","properties":{"any":{"description":"Title terms, ANY of which must appear in the person's current title. A multi-word term matches as a phrase, with at most one word between the words: `\"vp finance\"` matches \"VP Finance\" and \"VP of Finance\", not \"VP of Engineering, Finance partner\". Punctuation and case are ignored: `\"founder\"` matches \"Co-Founder & CEO\". A single common word matches across every function (`\"automation\"` finds manufacturing and procurement automation too) — prefer two-word terms and use `exclude`. An acronym is matched LITERALLY and expands to nothing: `\"cto\"` does not match \"Chief Technology Officer\", and the same role is written both ways inside one company, so send both forms — `[\"cto\", \"chief technology officer\"]`. At named companies (`companyIds`, `companyWebsites`) every term is searched again at each company, so a long list there can run out of time: send the forms the user means, not every variant.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"all":{"description":"Title terms that must ALL appear. Combine with `any` freely.","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"exclude":{"description":"Title terms that disqualify a person (`[\"assistant\", \"intern\"]`).","minItems":1,"maxItems":50,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}}},"additionalProperties":false},"startedSince":{"description":"Keeps moves whose new role STARTED in this month or later, read from `startedAt`. Works with `title`, `source` or `destination`, and with `detectedSince` and `seniority`: every filter sent applies. Month precision, because Trayo holds the start month, never the day: `2026-08` and `2026-08-25` both mean \"started in August 2026 or later\", so a \"last 30 days\" question gets the whole starting month back — check `startedAt` before calling a row inside a day-level window. Roles in the cutoff month cannot be confirmed inside a day-level last-30-days window. A role recorded with only a start year, or with no start date, does not match. Moves are still read from the 90-day detection window, so a month before that window finds only roles detected inside it. The start is checked after each move is read, so with a company a page reads a bounded number of recent moves and can come back short, even empty, with `hasMore: true`: follow `nextCursor`. A sampled source-only search cannot be paged, so `hasMore: true` there means narrow it.","type":"string","pattern":"^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])(-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01]))?$"},"startedBefore":{"description":"Exclusive upper bound on the start month, with source, destination or title. `2026-10` keeps roles started before October 2026. A full date uses its month. Combine with inclusive startedSince for adjacent, non-overlapping month slices. Year-only and missing start dates do not match; the 90-day detection window still applies.","type":"string","pattern":"^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])(-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01]))?$"},"destinationFilters":{"description":"With `title` only. Narrows the moves by the company they JOINED: `hq.countries` and `headcount`.","type":"object","properties":{"hq":{"type":"object","properties":{"countries":{"minItems":1,"maxItems":60,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":80},"description":"Countries of the headquarters of the company they JOINED: ISO-2 codes (`[\"US\", \"DE\"]`) or country names (`[\"United States\", \"Germany\"]`), any case. A name is read as its ISO-2 code. A company with no recorded country does not match."}},"required":["countries"],"additionalProperties":false},"headcount":{"description":"Inclusive employee-count band of the company they JOINED, e.g. `{\"min\": 500}`. A company with no recorded size does not match.","type":"object","properties":{"min":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":10000000},"max":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10000000}},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false},"JobChangeSearchResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"source":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"companyId":{"type":"string","description":"Trayo's id for the company. Send it back as `companyId` to name this exact company again."},"name":{"type":"string","description":"The company as Trayo records it."},"website":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company's website domain as Trayo files it."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company's LinkedIn page, when Trayo has it."},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The workspace account for this company: the one you sent, or the account that already holds its website or LinkedIn page. Null when the company is not in your workspace."},"resolvedBy":{"type":"string","enum":["account_match","company_id","linkedin","website","redirect","name"],"description":"Which key identified the company — the same resolver the Trayo app uses, in its order. `account_match`: the account's existing match. `linkedin`: the LinkedIn page, as sent or as spelled by a website sent on its own. `website`: the website domain. `redirect`: the website, after following a cross-domain redirect to the company's current site. `name`: the name, the weakest key.\n\nA match is exact on the key you sent, which is only the company you meant if the key was. A slug or name can belong to a different company than the one you mean, and the answer will then describe that company. Read `name` and `website` back before acting, and prefer `website` as the key — a domain resolves to whoever owns it."},"nameMatch":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["exact","approximate"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Set only when `resolvedBy` is `name`, null otherwise. `exact`: the company's name equals the name looked up once legal suffixes (Inc, GmbH, Ltd) are dropped. `approximate`: the closest name Trayo holds, which can be a different company (\"CR Management Consulting\" answered with \"m3 management consulting\"). Check an `approximate` match against what you know of the company before using it, and leave it out when it does not fit."}},"required":["companyId","name","website","linkedinUrl","accountId","resolvedBy","nameMatch"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The `source` company you sent, as resolved. Null when you sent none."},"destination":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"companyId":{"type":"string","description":"Trayo's id for the company. Send it back as `companyId` to name this exact company again."},"name":{"type":"string","description":"The company as Trayo records it."},"website":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company's website domain as Trayo files it."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company's LinkedIn page, when Trayo has it."},"accountId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The workspace account for this company: the one you sent, or the account that already holds its website or LinkedIn page. Null when the company is not in your workspace."},"resolvedBy":{"type":"string","enum":["account_match","company_id","linkedin","website","redirect","name"],"description":"Which key identified the company — the same resolver the Trayo app uses, in its order. `account_match`: the account's existing match. `linkedin`: the LinkedIn page, as sent or as spelled by a website sent on its own. `website`: the website domain. `redirect`: the website, after following a cross-domain redirect to the company's current site. `name`: the name, the weakest key.\n\nA match is exact on the key you sent, which is only the company you meant if the key was. A slug or name can belong to a different company than the one you mean, and the answer will then describe that company. Read `name` and `website` back before acting, and prefer `website` as the key — a domain resolves to whoever owns it."},"nameMatch":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["exact","approximate"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Set only when `resolvedBy` is `name`, null otherwise. `exact`: the company's name equals the name looked up once legal suffixes (Inc, GmbH, Ltd) are dropped. `approximate`: the closest name Trayo holds, which can be a different company (\"CR Management Consulting\" answered with \"m3 management consulting\"). Check an `approximate` match against what you know of the company before using it, and leave it out when it does not fit."}},"required":["companyId","name","website","linkedinUrl","accountId","resolvedBy","nameMatch"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The `destination` company you sent, as resolved. Null when you sent none."},"jobChanges":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"person":{"type":"object","properties":{"fullName":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's name, as Trayo holds it."},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their title in the role they joined."},"headline":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn headline."},"location":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Where they are based, as Trayo holds it."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Their LinkedIn profile. Send it straight to `POST /v1/contacts/lookup` or `POST /v1/research/person`."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The handle from that profile URL."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."}},"required":["fullName","title","headline","location","linkedinUrl","linkedinUsername","photoUrl"],"additionalProperties":false},"source":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company, as Trayo records it."},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The title the person held there."}},"required":["name","title"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The company they left, and their title there."},"destination":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company, as Trayo records it."},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The title the person held there."}},"required":["name","title"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"The company they joined, and their title there."},"detectedAt":{"type":"string","format":"date-time","pattern":"^(?:(?:\\d\\d[2468][048]|\\d\\d[13579][26]|\\d\\d0[48]|[02468][048]00|[13579][26]00)-02-29|\\d{4}-(?:(?:0[13578]|1[02])-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])|(?:0[469]|11)-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|30)|(?:02)-(?:0[1-9]|1\\d|2[0-8])))T(?:(?:[01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d(?::[0-5]\\d(?:\\.\\d+)?)?(?:Z))$","description":"When Trayo first observed the new role — not when the person started it. Rows come back most recently detected first."},"startedAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"When the person's role STARTED, as Trayo records it — `detectedAt` is when we saw it, and the two can be months apart. A PARTIAL date, not a timestamp: `2026-03` for a month we hold, or `2026` when we hold only a year. Null when we record no start date for the destination role, which is common. Both forms sort and compare as text (`startedAt >= '2026-08'`), and a year-only value never satisfies a month bound. Order the response by this field yourself if you want it — rows arrive ordered by `detectedAt`."},"startedAtPrecision":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["month","year"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"How precise `startedAt` is: `month` for `2026-08`, `year` for `2026`, null when `startedAt` is null. Never `day`: Trayo does not hold the day a role started."}},"required":["person","source","destination","detectedAt","startedAt","startedAtPrecision"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"The moves that matched, most recently detected first, up to `limit` (25 by default, 100 at most). ONE ROW PER PERSON PER DESTINATION: a person is reported once per destination company however many times we heard about the move — so a person who changed jobs twice inside the window still comes back as two rows, one per destination. A `title` search returns one row per person: their newest move into a matching role. An empty array is a successful search that matched nothing in the bounded search; it does not prove nobody moved. Every row is a move BETWEEN two companies: a promotion or title change inside one company is never a row here. For promotions, watch the company with the workspace `job_change` signal instead (`create_signal`, then `run_discovery`)."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"The page or its bounded scan filled, so more matches may remain. Send `nextCursor` back as `cursor`, with the same search, to get them. It reads the raw row count BEFORE duplicate records are collapsed, so a page can come back short — fewer rows than `limit` — and still be `true`. That is deliberate: it reports truncation rather than hiding it. Follow `hasMore` and `nextCursor`, never the row count. A sampled source-only search cannot be paged, so `true` there means narrow the search instead. A `title` search, and a company search with `startedSince` or `startedBefore`, and any indexed source-only search, reads a bounded number of moves per page, so a page can come back short, even empty, and still be `true`: follow the cursor. `hasMore: false` does not prove completeness, even when `sampled` is false: every search reads a bounded slice of recent moves before filtering, so matching moves can exist outside that slice."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Send this `nextCursor` back as `cursor`, verbatim and with the SAME search, for the next page. Null when there is no next page. The walk resumes where this page stopped, so a later page neither repeats nor skips what an earlier one returned, even when new moves are detected while you page. Null on a sampled source-only search, which reads a fixed sample and cannot be paged. A short page can still carry one, so follow the cursor rather than counting rows."},"sampled":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the search used a sample. `true`: only `source` was sent and the search read a small fixed sample of recent moves and kept the ones that left that company. An empty answer does not mean nobody left; retrying gives the same answer while the underlying data is unchanged. `false` with only `source`: the search walked the newest moves out of that company instead of sampling them. `false`: `destination` was sent, with or without `source`; the search read a bounded slice of the newest moves into that company before filtering by source. `hasMore: false` does not prove completeness with either value of `sampled`; matching moves may be outside the slice. With `title`, `true` means more people hold that title than the search reads, so it looked at part of them: narrow the title or add `all` or `exclude` terms rather than treating the answer as complete (`destinationFilters` and `startedSince` filter what was read and do not widen it). Promotions and same-company title changes never appear here. For hires and promotions at a watched company, use the workspace `job_change` signal (`create_signal`, then `run_discovery`). That signal is not a departure search and does not provide a complete list of who left."}},"required":["source","destination","jobChanges","hasMore","nextCursor","sampled"],"additionalProperties":false},"PostSearchBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"topic":{"description":"What the posts should be about. Omit when searching all posts by an author.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":300},"author":{"description":"Exact LinkedIn profile URL or handle of the post author. Use alone for their latest posts, or with topic. A person's name as topic does not filter authors. A valid handle format does not verify the person's identity.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2048},"windowDays":{"default":30,"description":"How many days back to search when publishedFrom and publishedTo are omitted, 1 to 30; default 30.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":30},"publishedFrom":{"description":"First publication date to include, YYYY-MM-DD. Send with publishedTo; overrides windowDays.","type":"string","pattern":"^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$"},"publishedTo":{"description":"Last publication date to include, YYYY-MM-DD. Send with publishedFrom; overrides windowDays.","type":"string","pattern":"^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$"},"limit":{"default":25,"description":"Maximum posts per page. Follow nextCursor with the same search filters until hasMore is false; page size may change.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50},"cursor":{"description":"The previous nextCursor, verbatim with the same topic, author and date/window inputs. Effective dates stay fixed across pages. Author pages are a live traversal; topic pages use a bounded ranked snapshot.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":1024}},"additionalProperties":false},"PostSearchResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"windowDays":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The relative window searched, or null for explicit publication dates."},"since":{"type":"string","description":"The earliest publication date searched, YYYY-MM-DD."},"until":{"type":"string","description":"The last publication date searched, YYYY-MM-DD."},"hasMore":{"type":"boolean","description":"More rows remain in this traversal. For topic searches, false means the bounded ranked candidate pool ended, not that all matching stored posts were found."},"nextCursor":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Send back as cursor with the same search filters. Null at the end. Do not compute or modify it."},"coverage":{"type":"object","properties":{"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["author_live","ranked_snapshot"]},"scope":{"type":"string","enum":["stored_matches","bounded_ranked_candidates"]},"candidateLimit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},{"type":"null"}]},"snapshotExpiresAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["mode","scope","candidateLimit","snapshotExpiresAt"],"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Author-only pages traverse current stored matches and can change across edits. Topic pages freeze a bounded ranked pool until expiry; neither guarantees full LinkedIn coverage."},"posts":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"postId":{"type":"string","description":"The LinkedIn post id. A string because it does not fit a JSON number. Send it, or `url`, as `post` to POST /v1/posts/engagers."},"url":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The post on LinkedIn."},"publishedAt":{"type":"string","description":"Publication date, YYYY-MM-DD."},"text":{"type":"string","description":"The post text, cut at 600 characters."},"author":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"fullName":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's name. The same value as `name`, under the name every person-returning route uses."},"headline":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"title":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The author's current job title, where we know it."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The author's LinkedIn profile URL."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The handle from that profile URL."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field under this name."}},"required":["name","fullName","headline","title","linkedinUrl","linkedinUsername","photoUrl"],"additionalProperties":false},"company":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"companyId":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Company dataset ID, where known. Reuse it as `companyId` in POST /v1/accounts/lookalikes. Send it as `company.companyId` to company research, stakeholder search or job-change search to name this exact company. Null when Trayo holds no company record for the employer: `name` is then the free text from the LinkedIn profile, and looking it up by name will usually miss; search for it with a find companies `query` instead."},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"website":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The company's LinkedIn page, when Trayo holds a record for it."},"employees":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Employee count, where known."},"country":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["companyId","name","website","linkedinUrl","employees","country"],"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The author's current company, or null when it is not known."},"commentCount":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991,"description":"Comments we hold for this post."}},"required":["postId","url","publishedAt","text","author","company","commentCount"],"additionalProperties":false},"description":"Topic matches rank best first; author-only posts sort newest first. Empty means no stored matches for the requested identity, dates and topic; it does not verify the identity or prove that no posts were published. Nothing is saved to your workspace."}},"required":["windowDays","since","until","hasMore","nextCursor","coverage","posts"],"additionalProperties":false},"PostEngagersBodyDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"post":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":2048,"pattern":"^\\d{15,20}$|activity[-:]\\d{15,20}|linkedin\\.com\\/posts\\/[a-z0-9_-]+-share-\\d{15,20}-[a-z0-9_-]+","description":"A LinkedIn activity or share post URL, or the `postId` from a post search. Share URLs resolve only when Trayo holds the post. For a share or ugcPost URN, send the post's /posts/ URL instead; its number is not an activity ID."}},"required":["post"],"additionalProperties":false},"PostEngagersResponseDto":{"type":"object","properties":{"postId":{"type":"string"},"url":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"indexed":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether Trayo found the post. False is not evidence that the post has no comments."},"reason":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["outside_coverage","not_held"]},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Why `indexed` is false, or null when it is true. `outside_coverage`: the post was published before `coverageFrom`, so Trayo cannot hold it. `not_held`: the post is inside coverage, but Trayo does not hold it."},"coverageFrom":{"type":"string","description":"The earliest publication date Trayo keeps posts for, YYYY-MM-DD. It moves forward daily."},"commentsChecked":{"type":"boolean","description":"True when the engagers come from Trayo's comment store for this post. False when Trayo holds the post but not its comment thread, or does not hold the post: empty commenters then say nothing about how many people commented."},"commentsLoadedAt":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Latest Trayo load time among the returned stored comments, ISO 8601. Null means no stored comment rows. Source data may be older than this time; even with a date, other LinkedIn comments or replies may be missing."},"engagers":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"role":{"type":"string","enum":["author","commenter"]},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"fullName":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's name. The same value as `name`, under the name every person-returning route uses."},"linkedinUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's LinkedIn profile URL."},"linkedinUsername":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The handle from that profile URL."},"photoUrl":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"The person's profile photo, as a directly loadable image URL. Null when Trayo holds no real photo for them — show initials then. Every route that returns a person carries this field und